{"version":"1.0.0","disclaimer":"General safety information, not medical advice. Do not start, stop or change any medicine or herbal product without talking to your doctor or pharmacist.","drugClassLabels":{"anticoagulant_vka":"Blood thinner (warfarin-type)","anticoagulant_doac":"Blood thinner (newer oral)","anticoagulant_heparin":"Blood thinner (heparin)","antiplatelet":"Antiplatelet","nsaid":"Painkiller (NSAID)","antidiabetic_insulin":"Insulin","antidiabetic_sulfonylurea":"Diabetes medicine (sulfonylurea)","antidiabetic_other":"Diabetes medicine","antihypertensive":"Blood pressure medicine","diuretic_loop_thiazide":"Water tablet (diuretic)","potassium_sparing":"Potassium-raising medicine","cardiac_glycoside":"Digoxin","antiarrhythmic":"Heart rhythm medicine","nitrate_pde5":"Nitrate / PDE5 inhibitor","statin":"Statin","thyroid_hormone":"Thyroid hormone","antithyroid":"Antithyroid medicine","immunosuppressant":"Immunosuppressant","corticosteroid":"Steroid","antidepressant_ssri_snri":"Antidepressant (SSRI/SNRI)","antidepressant_maoi":"Antidepressant (MAOI)","antidepressant_tca":"Antidepressant (tricyclic)","sedative_hypnotic":"Sedative / sleeping pill","antiepileptic":"Anti-seizure medicine","antipsychotic":"Antipsychotic","lithium":"Lithium","opioid":"Opioid","anticancer":"Cancer medicine","antiretroviral":"HIV medicine","antibiotic_chelating":"Antibiotic (binds minerals)","antibiotic_other":"Antibiotic","antitubercular":"TB medicine","hepatotoxic":"Medicine that can strain the liver","nephrotoxic":"Medicine that can strain the kidneys","hormonal_contraceptive_estrogen":"Hormonal contraceptive / oestrogen","ppi_antacid":"Acid reducer","iron_mineral_supplement":"Iron / mineral","laxative_stimulant":"Stimulant laxative","theophylline":"Theophylline","anaesthesia_surgery":"Anaesthesia / surgery","cyp3a4_substrate_narrow":"Processed by CYP3A4 (narrow margin)","cyp2c9_substrate":"Processed by CYP2C9","cyp2d6_substrate":"Processed by CYP2D6","cyp1a2_substrate":"Processed by CYP1A2","cyp2c19_substrate":"Processed by CYP2C19","pgp_substrate":"Moved by P-gp transporter","antiparkinson_levodopa":"Parkinson's medicine (levodopa)","photosensitizing":"Causes sun sensitivity","anticholinergic":"Anticholinergic"},"herbs":[{"id":"ashwagandha","name":"Ashwagandha","nameHi":"अश्वगंधा","sanskrit":"Ashvagandha (अश्वगन्धा)","latin":"Withania somnifera (L.) Dunal","otherNames":["Indian ginseng","Winter cherry"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda as a rasayana for general vitality and the wellbeing of the nervous system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Ashwagandha churna (root powder)","Ashwagandharishta","Ashwagandha capsules/tablets (e.g. KSM-66, Sensoril extracts)","Ashwagandha gummies","Ashwagandhadi lehya"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"NCCIH advises avoiding ashwagandha during pregnancy and not using it while breastfeeding.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Rare but sometimes severe liver injury (usually cholestatic, appearing within weeks to months) has been reported with ashwagandha supplements in several countries.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548536/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31991029/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37756041/"]},{"flag":"thyroid","note":"May raise thyroid hormone levels; cases of thyrotoxicosis and painless thyroiditis have been reported. NCCIH advises against use in people with thyroid disorders.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28829155/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35475098/"]},{"flag":"autoimmune","note":"NCCIH advises against use in people with autoimmune conditions (immune-stimulating activity).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha"]},{"flag":"surgery","note":"NCCIH advises against use by people about to have surgery (possible additive sedation with anaesthesia).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Short-term use (up to about 3 months) appears reasonably safe; long-term safety is not established.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise thyroid hormone levels and add to the effect of levothyroxine, so thyroid levels may move out of range.","mechanism":"Increases serum T3/T4 in a randomised trial; case reports of thyrotoxicosis.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, your doctor may want to re-check thyroid blood tests.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28829155/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35475098/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antithyroid","severity":"moderate","effect":"May push thyroid hormone levels up and work against antithyroid medicines.","mechanism":"Thyroid-stimulating effect (raised T3/T4 in trial; thyrotoxicosis case reports).","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35475098/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38559552/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"moderate","effect":"May work against medicines that dampen the immune system.","mechanism":"Immune-stimulating activity; NCCIH lists immunosuppressants among possible interactions.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"sedative_hypnotic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to drowsiness from sleeping pills or sedatives.","mechanism":"GABA-mimetic / sedative activity in preclinical studies; NCCIH lists sedatives among possible interactions.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37335157/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May change how well anti-seizure medicines work or add to drowsiness.","mechanism":"NCCIH lists anticonvulsants among possible interactions; GABA-ergic activity shown in animals.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24117067/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further when taken with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect seen in animal studies and small clinical studies; NCCIH lists diabetes medicines among possible interactions.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31975514/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further when taken with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect seen in animal studies and small clinical studies; NCCIH lists diabetes medicines among possible interactions.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31975514/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further when taken with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect seen in animal studies and small clinical studies; NCCIH lists diabetes medicines among possible interactions.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31975514/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood pressure further when taken with blood-pressure medicines.","mechanism":"NCCIH lists blood-pressure medicines among possible interactions; mechanism not well defined.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for dizziness or light-headedness.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the risk of liver injury when taken with medicines that can harm the liver.","mechanism":"Ashwagandha itself is a rare cause of drug-induced liver injury; additive risk is theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548536/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31991029/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_ssri_snri","severity":"minor","effect":"Side effects such as stomach upset, muscle pain or diarrhoea were reported when combined with some antidepressants.","mechanism":"Reported in a retrospective pharmacovigilance chart review (sertraline, escitalopram, paroxetine); mechanism unclear.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37829299/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anaesthesia_surgery","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to sedation from anaesthesia.","mechanism":"NCCIH advises against use before surgery; sedative activity.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Tell your surgeon, anaesthetist and dentist that you take it. It is commonly advised to stop the herb about 2 weeks before planned surgery; ask your doctor.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"turmeric","name":"Turmeric (incl. curcumin supplements)","nameHi":"हल्दी","sanskrit":"Haridra (हरिद्रा)","latin":"Curcuma longa L.","otherNames":["Curcumin","Indian saffron"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and Indian cooking for the general wellbeing of the skin, digestion and joints.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Haldi powder (culinary)","Curcumin capsules (often with piperine/black pepper)","Bioavailable curcumin formulations (e.g. Meriva, BCM-95, Theracurmin)","Haridrakhand","Turmeric latte / golden milk mixes"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Liver injury (often hepatocellular, sometimes autoimmune-like) has been reported with turmeric/curcumin supplements, especially highly bioavailable or piperine-enhanced products; amounts used in food are not a concern.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/turmeric","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548561/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36252717/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32656820/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Supplement doses may be unsafe in pregnancy; EMA does not recommend medicinal use in pregnancy or breastfeeding.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/turmeric","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-curcuma-longa-l-rhizoma-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"other","note":"EMA advises against medicinal use with bile-duct obstruction, gallstones or liver disease (stimulates bile flow / gallbladder contraction).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-curcuma-longa-l-rhizoma-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10102956/"]},{"flag":"bleeding","note":"Curcumin has antiplatelet and anticoagulant effects in laboratory studies.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29052850/"]},{"flag":"heavy_metals","note":"Loose turmeric powder has been found adulterated with lead chromate in South Asia and linked to lead exposure, including in the US.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31550596/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28358991/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"High-dose turmeric has been linked to iron-deficiency anaemia in a case report (curcumin binds iron).","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30899609/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase the effect of warfarin-type blood thinners and raise the risk of bleeding (raised INR reported).","mechanism":"Antiplatelet/anticoagulant activity of curcumin; a probable case of raised INR with a vitamin K antagonist (fluindione).","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery. Your INR may need to be checked more often.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25230280/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29052850/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the bleeding risk of newer blood thinners.","mechanism":"Curcumin antiplatelet/anticoagulant activity in lab studies; additive effect is theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29052850/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the effect of antiplatelet medicines and increase bleeding risk.","mechanism":"Curcumin inhibits platelet aggregation in lab studies; in rats it changed clopidogrel levels without changing platelet effect.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29052850/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23807811/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"nsaid","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to bleeding risk, including stomach bleeding, with NSAID painkillers.","mechanism":"Additive antiplatelet effect; theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29052850/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise tacrolimus blood levels (kidney harm reported in one case), although another monitored patient showed no change.","mechanism":"Probable CYP3A4/P-gp inhibition by curcumin; raised tacrolimus levels in rats and one case report; a second case found no effect at culinary doses.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you take tacrolimus or cyclosporine, levels may need extra monitoring.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28104136/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22123127/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37586787/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticancer","severity":"moderate","effect":"Curcumin lowered blood levels of tamoxifen and its active form endoxifen, which might reduce its effect.","mechanism":"Clinical PK study in breast-cancer patients: endoxifen AUC fell 7.7% with curcumin and 12.4% with curcumin + piperine.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Tell your cancer team about any curcumin supplement.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30909366/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the glucose-lowering effect of glibenclamide (glyburide).","mechanism":"Small clinical study: curcumin altered glyburide levels and lowered glucose further (no hypoglycaemia observed).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25044423/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"Curcumin reduced absorption of the beta-blocker talinolol in volunteers, which could reduce its effect.","mechanism":"Clinical PK study: talinolol AUC fell about one third after 6 days of curcumin (transporter-mediated).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17468862/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"pgp_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"May change blood levels of medicines that are moved by P-gp/BCRP transporters.","mechanism":"Curcumin altered talinolol (P-gp) and sulfasalazine (BCRP) exposure in human studies.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17468862/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22300367/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the risk of liver injury with other medicines that can affect the liver.","mechanism":"Turmeric/curcumin is itself linked to rare liver injury; additive risk theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548561/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36252717/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"iron_mineral_supplement","severity":"minor","effect":"High doses may reduce iron absorption.","mechanism":"Curcumin is an iron chelator; iron-deficiency anaemia reported with high-dose turmeric.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Ask your doctor or pharmacist; consider taking iron at a different time of day from turmeric supplements.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30899609/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16545682/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anaesthesia_surgery","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to bleeding risk around surgery.","mechanism":"Antiplatelet activity; theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Tell your surgeon, anaesthetist and dentist that you take it. It is commonly advised to stop the herb about 2 weeks before planned surgery; ask your doctor.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29052850/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"guggul","name":"Guggul","nameHi":"गुग्गुल","sanskrit":"Guggulu (गुग्गुलु)","latin":"Commiphora wightii (Arn.) Bhandari (syn. Commiphora mukul)","otherNames":["Indian bdellium","Gugulipid / guggulsterone"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, usually as purified resin in guggulu formulations, for the general wellbeing of joints and metabolism.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Shuddha guggulu","Yogaraj guggulu","Kaishore guggulu","Triphala guggulu","Gugulipid / guggul extract capsules"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"allergy","note":"Skin rash (hypersensitivity) was noticeably more common with guggulipid than placebo in a randomised trial.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12915429/"]},{"flag":"thyroid","note":"Guggulsterone stimulated thyroid function in rats; relevance in people is uncertain.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6739577/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Guggulsterone binds steroid hormone receptors (oestrogen, progesterone and others); use in pregnancy is not supported by safety data.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15602004/"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Acute liver failure has been reported with a multi-ingredient 'fat burner' that contained guggul (cause not attributable to guggul alone).","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21499580/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"moderate","effect":"May reduce absorption and effect of propranolol and diltiazem.","mechanism":"Single 1 g dose of gugulipid significantly reduced Cmax and AUC of propranolol and diltiazem in healthy volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, have your blood pressure and heart rate checked.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7852226/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiarrhythmic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May reduce blood levels of diltiazem and beta-blockers used for heart rhythm.","mechanism":"Reduced bioavailability of diltiazem and propranolol in healthy volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7852226/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP3A4, making them less effective.","mechanism":"Guggulsterone activates the pregnane X receptor and induces CYP3A in lab studies; not confirmed in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15075359/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"minor","effect":"May change thyroid function tests or levothyroxine needs.","mechanism":"Thyroid-stimulating action of Z-guggulsterone in rats.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6739577/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antithyroid","severity":"minor","effect":"May work against antithyroid medicines.","mechanism":"Thyroid-stimulating action of guggulsterone in rats; theoretical.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6739577/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hormonal_contraceptive_estrogen","severity":"minor","effect":"May interfere with hormone-based medicines.","mechanism":"Guggulsterone binds oestrogen, progesterone and other steroid receptors in vitro; clinical relevance unknown.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15602004/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"giloy","name":"Giloy / Heart-leaved moonseed","nameHi":"गिलोय","sanskrit":"Guduchi (गुडूची), Amrita","latin":"Tinospora cordifolia (Willd.) Miers","otherNames":["Guduchi","Gulvel"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda as a rasayana for general vitality and the wellbeing of the body's natural defences.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Giloy juice","Giloy ghanvati / tablets","Guduchi satva","Giloy-tulsi immunity drops","Samshamani vati"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Multicentre case series from India (especially during COVID-19 'immunity booster' use) linked giloy to liver injury, often autoimmune-like. The Ministry of Ayush disputes the link, citing possible plant misidentification.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608429/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35037744/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34230786/","https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1733260"]},{"flag":"autoimmune","note":"May unmask or trigger autoimmune-like hepatitis; caution in people with autoimmune conditions.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35068778/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35037744/"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Glucose-lowering activity in animal studies.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21665451/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the risk of liver injury with medicines that can affect the liver.","mechanism":"Giloy linked to autoimmune-like liver injury in case series; additive risk theoretical.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report tiredness, dark urine or yellow eyes promptly.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35037744/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608429/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"moderate","effect":"May work against medicines that dampen the immune system.","mechanism":"Immune-stimulating activity; associated with autoimmune-like hepatitis. Interaction itself is theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35068778/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24105360/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise glibenclamide levels and lower blood sugar further.","mechanism":"In rats, 14-day giloy extract increased glibenclamide bioavailability and reduced clearance; CYP2C9 inhibition in vitro.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29413979/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Hypoglycaemic activity of giloy alkaloids in animals.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21665451/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Hypoglycaemic activity of giloy alkaloids in animals.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21665451/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly raise levels of medicines broken down by CYP3A4.","mechanism":"Mild CYP3A4 inhibition by stem extract in vitro (stronger for its protoberberine alkaloid fraction); not shown in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24105360/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27721546/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly raise levels of medicines broken down by CYP2C9.","mechanism":"CYP2C9 inhibition in human liver microsomes at high concentrations; not shown in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27721546/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29413979/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c19_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly raise levels of medicines broken down by CYP2C19.","mechanism":"CYP2C19 inhibited by giloy extract in rat and human liver microsomes (weaker than CYP2C9/CYP2D6); not shown in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29413979/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"tulsi","name":"Holy basil","nameHi":"तुलसी","sanskrit":"Tulasi (तुलसी)","latin":"Ocimum tenuiflorum L. (syn. Ocimum sanctum L.)","otherNames":["Holy basil","Krishna tulsi","Rama tulsi"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and Indian households for the general wellbeing of the respiratory system and stress resilience.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Tulsi tea","Tulsi drops","Tulsi capsules","Tulsi leaves (fresh)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Antifertility effects have been reported in animal studies; safety in pregnancy is not established.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4344433/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28400848/"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Lowered fasting and post-meal blood glucose in a small randomised trial.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8880292/"]},{"flag":"bleeding","note":"Tulsi fixed oil prolonged clotting time in animals.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11694358/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in a small randomised placebo-controlled trial.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8880292/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28400848/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in a small randomised placebo-controlled trial.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8880292/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28400848/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in a small randomised placebo-controlled trial.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8880292/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28400848/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the effect of blood thinners.","mechanism":"Prolonged blood clotting time in animals; clinical relevance unknown.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11694358/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Prolonged clotting time in animals; theoretical.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11694358/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"sedative_hypnotic","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to drowsiness from sedatives.","mechanism":"Tulsi fixed oil prolonged pentobarbitone sleeping time in animals.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11694358/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood pressure further.","mechanism":"Tulsi fixed oil lowered blood pressure in animals.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11694358/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"brahmi","name":"Brahmi / Water hyssop","nameHi":"ब्राह्मी","sanskrit":"Brahmi (ब्राह्मी)","latin":"Bacopa monnieri (L.) Wettst.","otherNames":["Bacopa","Jalnim"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda as a medhya rasayana for the general wellbeing of memory and the nervous system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Brahmi churna","Brahmi vati","Brahmi ghrita","Bacopa extract capsules (e.g. Bacognize, CDRI-08)","Brahmi oil (topical)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Stomach upset (nausea, cramps, loose stools) is the most common side effect in trials.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20590480/"]},{"flag":"thyroid","note":"Raised T4 levels in mice, suggesting a thyroid-stimulating effect.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12065164/"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Not linked to clinically apparent liver injury in LiverTox.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK603563/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP3A4.","mechanism":"Bacopa extract inhibited CYP3A4 (and CYP2C9, CYP2C19, CYP1A2) in vitro; estimated gut concentrations could be relevant. Not tested in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24566323/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP2C9 (e.g. some blood thinners and diabetes tablets).","mechanism":"Non-competitive CYP2C9 inhibition in vitro; not tested in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24566323/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c19_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP2C19 (e.g. omeprazole, citalopram) and could reduce activation of clopidogrel.","mechanism":"CYP2C19 was the most strongly inhibited enzyme in vitro (IC50 ~24 µg/mL, non-competitive); not tested in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24566323/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp1a2_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP1A2.","mechanism":"CYP1A2 inhibition in vitro.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24566323/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"minor","effect":"May change thyroid hormone levels.","mechanism":"Raised T4 in mice.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12065164/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_maoi","severity":"minor","effect":"A serious cardiac event was reported in a patient combining Bacopa with moclobemide.","mechanism":"Single report in a pharmacovigilance chart review; causality and mechanism uncertain.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37829299/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"shatavari","name":"Shatavari / Wild asparagus","nameHi":"शतावरी","sanskrit":"Shatavari (शतावरी)","latin":"Asparagus racemosus Willd.","otherNames":["Satavar"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda as a rasayana for the general wellbeing of the female reproductive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Shatavari churna","Shatavari granules / kalpa","Shatavari capsules","Shatavari ghrita"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Used traditionally around childbirth and as a galactagogue, but there is little safety data for pregnancy or breastfeeding.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23012383/"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Stimulated insulin secretion and lowered glucose in animal and cell studies.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17210753/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21899804/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Insulin-secretory and glucose-lowering effects in animal and cell studies.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17210753/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21899804/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Insulin-secretory and glucose-lowering effects in animal and cell studies.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17210753/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21899804/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Insulin-secretory and glucose-lowering effects in animal and cell studies.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17210753/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21899804/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"diuretic_loop_thiazide","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the fluid-losing effect of diuretics.","mechanism":"Diuretic activity of root extract in rats; theoretical in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20931905/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"arjuna","name":"Arjuna","nameHi":"अर्जुन","sanskrit":"Arjuna (अर्जुन)","latin":"Terminalia arjuna (Roxb. ex DC.) Wight & Arn.","otherNames":["Arjun bark"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, as bark decoctions, for the general wellbeing of the heart and circulation.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Arjuna bark powder (churna)","Arjunarishta","Arjuna ksheerapaka (milk decoction)","Arjuna capsules/tablets"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"bleeding","note":"Bark extract inhibited platelet activation in vitro.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19437336/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Clinical safety data are short-term and limited.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24600529/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25014508/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP3A4.","mechanism":"Bark extracts strongly inhibited CYP3A4 in human liver microsomes (IC50 < 50 µg/mL); not tested in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28962416/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP2C9, such as warfarin.","mechanism":"CYP2C9 inhibition in human liver microsomes; not tested in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28962416/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2d6_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP2D6, such as many beta-blockers and antidepressants.","mechanism":"CYP2D6 inhibition in human liver microsomes; not tested in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28962416/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Inhibited platelet aggregation and activation in vitro.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19437336/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the effect of warfarin and raise bleeding risk.","mechanism":"Theoretical: antiplatelet activity plus CYP2C9 inhibition in vitro.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19437336/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28962416/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the effect of heart and blood-pressure medicines.","mechanism":"Used alongside anti-anginal drugs in small trials; additive effects are theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12086380/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24600529/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"neem","name":"Neem","nameHi":"नीम","sanskrit":"Nimba (निम्ब)","latin":"Azadirachta indica A.Juss.","otherNames":["Margosa","Indian lilac"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, mostly as leaf preparations, for the general wellbeing of the skin.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Neem leaf capsules/tablets","Neem leaf juice","Nimbadi churna","Neem oil (topical; poisonous if swallowed)","Neem toothpaste/datun"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"children","note":"Swallowing neem (margosa) oil has caused serious poisoning in infants and young children, including a Reye-like illness with brain swelling, liver damage and deaths. Neem oil must never be given by mouth to children.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6110100/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7330956/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18250509/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Antifertility and contraceptive effects in animals; avoid in pregnancy or when trying to conceive.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34092456/"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Leaf extract lowered blood glucose in animal studies.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10919098/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Hypoglycaemic effect of leaf extract in normal and diabetic animals; human data limited.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10919098/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26716795/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Hypoglycaemic effect of leaf extract in normal and diabetic animals; human data limited.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10919098/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26716795/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Hypoglycaemic effect of leaf extract in normal and diabetic animals; human data limited.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10919098/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26716795/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"minor","effect":"May work against medicines that dampen the immune system.","mechanism":"Immune-stimulating activity of neem preparations in animals; theoretical.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1452404/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3302545/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"amla","name":"Indian gooseberry","nameHi":"आंवला","sanskrit":"Amalaki (आमलकी)","latin":"Phyllanthus emblica L. (syn. Emblica officinalis Gaertn.)","otherNames":["Amalaki","Emblic"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda as a rasayana for general vitality and the wellbeing of digestion.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Amla juice","Amla churna","Amla murabba / candy","Chyawanprash (main ingredient)","Triphala (one of three fruits)","Amla extract capsules (e.g. Capros)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"bleeding","note":"A standardised amla extract reduced platelet aggregation and prolonged bleeding time in clinical studies.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24291054/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25756303/"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Amla powder lowered blood glucose in a small human study.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21495900/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the effect of aspirin or clopidogrel and increase bleeding risk.","mechanism":"In a crossover study in diabetic patients, amla extract inhibited platelet aggregation and prolonged bleeding time alone and combined with clopidogrel or aspirin.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24291054/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the bleeding risk of warfarin-type blood thinners.","mechanism":"Antiplatelet activity shown clinically; combination with anticoagulants not studied.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24291054/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25756303/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the bleeding risk of newer blood thinners.","mechanism":"Antiplatelet activity; theoretical additive effect.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24291054/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in a small human study.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21495900/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in a small human study.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21495900/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in a small human study.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21495900/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood pressure slightly when added to blood-pressure medicines.","mechanism":"Add-on randomised trial showed modest blood-pressure lowering.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33570228/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"ginger","name":"Ginger","nameHi":"अदरक / सोंठ","sanskrit":"Shunthi (शुण्ठी); fresh: Ardraka (आर्द्रक)","latin":"Zingiber officinale Roscoe","otherNames":["Sonth","Dry ginger"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and Indian cooking for the general wellbeing of digestion.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Fresh ginger / adrak","Saunth powder","Ginger capsules","Ginger tea","Trikatu (with black pepper and pippali)","Adrak ras / ginger juice"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Supplements may be safe in pregnancy (NCCIH), but EMA prefers avoiding medicinal use in pregnancy as a precaution and does not recommend use while breastfeeding.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ginger","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-zingiber-officinale-roscoe-rhizoma-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"bleeding","note":"Effects on platelets are inconsistent across studies; an over-anticoagulation case with phenprocoumon has been reported.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14742762/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16883626/"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Not linked to clinically apparent liver injury on its own (LiverTox).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK600585/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise INR and bleeding risk with coumarin blood thinners in some people.","mechanism":"Case report of INR up to 10 with phenprocoumon; a controlled study in healthy volunteers found no effect on warfarin INR or kinetics.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery. Your INR may need to be checked if you start or stop ginger supplements.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14742762/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15801937/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to bleeding risk with newer blood thinners.","mechanism":"Possible antiplatelet effect; theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16883626/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Ginger inhibited platelet aggregation and had a synergistic antiplatelet effect with nifedipine in humans; other studies inconsistent.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16883626/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"nsaid","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to bleeding risk with NSAID painkillers.","mechanism":"Additive antiplatelet effect; theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16883626/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"With nifedipine, ginger increased the anti-clotting effect on platelets.","mechanism":"Synergistic antiplatelet effect of ginger plus nifedipine in hypertensive patients and volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16883626/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticancer","severity":"moderate","effect":"Ginger was linked to raised crizotinib levels and liver injury in a cancer patient.","mechanism":"Probable CYP3A4 inhibition; single case report.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Tell your cancer team about ginger supplements.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30701569/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"minor","effect":"May raise tacrolimus levels (shown in rats); a monitored patient showed no change at culinary doses.","mechanism":"Ginger juice increased tacrolimus AUC in rats; human case negative.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22123127/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37586787/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anaesthesia_surgery","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to bleeding risk around surgery.","mechanism":"Possible antiplatelet effect; theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Tell your surgeon, anaesthetist and dentist that you take it. It is commonly advised to stop the herb about 2 weeks before planned surgery; ask your doctor.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16883626/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"garlic","name":"Garlic","nameHi":"लहसुन","sanskrit":"Lashuna (लशुन)","latin":"Allium sativum L.","otherNames":["Rasona"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and Indian cooking for the general wellbeing of digestion and circulation.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Garlic (culinary)","Garlic pearls / garlic oil capsules","Aged garlic extract","Lashunadi vati"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"bleeding","note":"Garlic supplements may increase bleeding risk; spontaneous bleeding and post-operative bleeding have been reported.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garlic","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-allium-sativum-l-bulbus_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2352608/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7809259/"]},{"flag":"surgery","note":"EMA advises avoiding garlic preparations 7 days before surgery because of post-operative bleeding risk.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-allium-sativum-l-bulbus_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Supplement amounts may not be safe in pregnancy or breastfeeding; food amounts are fine.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garlic","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-allium-sativum-l-bulbus_en.pdf"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antiretroviral","severity":"major","effect":"Can sharply lower blood levels of some HIV medicines (saquinavir), risking treatment failure and resistance.","mechanism":"Garlic supplements cut saquinavir AUC by about half in volunteers (likely CYP3A4/P-gp induction); EMA contraindicates use with saquinavir/ritonavir.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid garlic supplements with HIV protease inhibitors unless your HIV doctor agrees. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-allium-sativum-l-bulbus_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11740713/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":["saquinavir"]},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the effect of warfarin and raise bleeding risk.","mechanism":"Antiplatelet effect; EMA and NCCIH advise caution. A trial of aged garlic extract with warfarin found no increased bleeding.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-allium-sativum-l-bulbus_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garlic","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16484565/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to bleeding risk with newer blood thinners.","mechanism":"EMA advises caution with oral anticoagulants (antiplatelet effect).","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-allium-sativum-l-bulbus_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_heparin","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to bleeding risk with heparin injections.","mechanism":"Antiplatelet effect; theoretical additive bleeding.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garlic","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22300597/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the effect of aspirin or clopidogrel and increase bleeding risk.","mechanism":"Garlic inhibits platelet function; EMA advises caution with antiplatelet therapy.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-allium-sativum-l-bulbus_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garlic","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2352608/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"nsaid","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to bleeding risk with NSAID painkillers.","mechanism":"Additive antiplatelet effect; theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garlic"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anaesthesia_surgery","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase bleeding during and after surgery.","mechanism":"Antiplatelet effect; EMA advises stopping 7 days before surgery.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Tell your surgeon and dentist. EMA advises stopping garlic supplements at least 7 days before surgery.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-allium-sativum-l-bulbus_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7809259/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antitubercular","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood levels of isoniazid.","mechanism":"Garlic extract reduced isoniazid bioavailability in rabbits; not studied in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16699292/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticancer","severity":"minor","effect":"May change docetaxel clearance in some people.","mechanism":"Clinical PK study found no significant change overall, but possible reduced clearance in CYP3A5*1A carriers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16899612/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Garlic supplements may modestly reduce blood glucose (NCCIH).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garlic"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Garlic supplements may modestly reduce blood glucose (NCCIH).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garlic"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Garlic supplements may modestly reduce blood glucose (NCCIH).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garlic"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood pressure slightly with blood-pressure medicines.","mechanism":"Small blood-pressure-lowering effect (NCCIH).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garlic"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"fenugreek","name":"Fenugreek","nameHi":"मेथी","sanskrit":"Methika (मेथिका)","latin":"Trigonella foenum-graecum L.","otherNames":["Methi dana"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and Indian cooking for the general wellbeing of digestion and metabolism.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Methi seeds / powder","Fenugreek capsules","Methi water","Lactation teas and supplements"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Not safe in pregnancy in amounts above food use; linked to birth defects in animals and people. EMA does not recommend it in pregnancy, breastfeeding, or women who may become pregnant without contraception.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/fenugreek","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/european-union-herbal-monograph-trigonella-foenum-graecum-l-semen-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Large doses may cause a harmful drop in blood sugar; EMA advises closer glucose monitoring in people on diabetes treatment.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/fenugreek","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/european-union-herbal-monograph-trigonella-foenum-graecum-l-semen-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"allergy","note":"EMA contraindicates use with allergy to peanut, soya or other legumes (cross-reactivity).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/european-union-herbal-monograph-trigonella-foenum-graecum-l-semen-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28283164/"]},{"flag":"thyroid","note":"Reduced T3 production in mice and rats.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10527654/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further and cause low blood sugar with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in meta-analyses of clinical trials; EMA advises close glycaemic monitoring.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/european-union-herbal-monograph-trigonella-foenum-graecum-l-semen-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/fenugreek","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24438170/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further and cause low blood sugar with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in meta-analyses of clinical trials; EMA advises close glycaemic monitoring.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/european-union-herbal-monograph-trigonella-foenum-graecum-l-semen-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/fenugreek","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24438170/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further and cause low blood sugar with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in meta-analyses of clinical trials; EMA advises close glycaemic monitoring.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/european-union-herbal-monograph-trigonella-foenum-graecum-l-semen-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/fenugreek","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24438170/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase the effect of warfarin (raised INR reported).","mechanism":"Case report of raised INR with a boldo-fenugreek combination; mechanism unclear (coumarin-like constituents proposed).","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery. Your INR may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11310527/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to liver stress from medicines that can harm the liver.","mechanism":"Case of worsened ribociclib liver toxicity with a fenugreek supplement; causality uncertain.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40388649/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548826/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"minor","effect":"May change thyroid hormone levels.","mechanism":"Inhibited T3 production in rodents.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10527654/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"nitrate_pde5","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower sildenafil levels.","mechanism":"Fenugreek reduced sildenafil Cmax and AUC in dogs; not studied in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24719213/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"karela","name":"Bitter melon / Bitter gourd","nameHi":"करेला","sanskrit":"Karavellaka (कारवेल्लक)","latin":"Momordica charantia L.","otherNames":["Bitter gourd","Balsam pear"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and Indian cooking for the general wellbeing of metabolism.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Karela juice","Karela-jamun juice","Karela powder / capsules","Karela (cooked vegetable)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Can lower blood sugar; hypoglycaemic coma and convulsions have been reported in children given bitter melon tea.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12625217/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK590483/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Seeds and fruit contain abortifacient proteins in animal studies; avoid in pregnancy.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12625217/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15182917/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Seeds contain vicine; people with G6PD deficiency may be at risk of haemolytic anaemia (favism-like).","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12625217/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with sulfonylureas.","mechanism":"Case of improved/excess glucose control when karela curry was eaten with chlorpropamide; additive hypoglycaemic effect.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/85186/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12625217/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect seen in trials (modest; evidence quality low).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21211558/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22895968/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect seen in trials (modest; evidence quality low).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21211558/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22895968/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"jamun","name":"Jamun / Java plum","nameHi":"जामुन","sanskrit":"Jambu (जम्बू)","latin":"Syzygium cumini (L.) Skeels","otherNames":["Jambolan","Black plum"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, especially seed powder, for the general wellbeing of metabolism and digestion.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Jamun seed powder (guthli churna)","Jamun juice / vinegar","Karela-jamun juice","Jamun fruit"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Seed and fruit extracts lower blood glucose in animal studies; human data are limited.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9687076/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23642956/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Hypoglycaemic activity of seed extract in animals; clinical data limited and older.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9687076/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23642956/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18380393/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Hypoglycaemic activity of seed extract in animals; clinical data limited and older.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9687076/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23642956/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18380393/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Hypoglycaemic activity of seed extract in animals; clinical data limited and older.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9687076/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23642956/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18380393/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"gurmar","name":"Gymnema","nameHi":"गुड़मार","sanskrit":"Meshashringi (मेषशृङ्गी)","latin":"Gymnema sylvestre (Retz.) R.Br. ex Sm.","otherNames":["Gurmar","Sugar destroyer"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for the general wellbeing of metabolism; the leaf briefly dulls sweet taste.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Gurmar churna","Gymnema capsules/tablets","Gurmar in polyherbal sugar-balance products (e.g. Madhunashini vati)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Lowers blood glucose; insulin requirements fell in people on insulin in an early study.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259216/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259217/"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Rare cases of liver injury have been reported.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK610217/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20856101/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further; insulin or tablet doses may need adjusting.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in clinical studies; reduced insulin requirements reported.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259216/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259217/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further; insulin or tablet doses may need adjusting.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in clinical studies; reduced insulin requirements reported.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259216/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259217/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further; insulin or tablet doses may need adjusting.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in clinical studies; reduced insulin requirements reported.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259216/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259217/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May change blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP2C9 (e.g. tolbutamide, warfarin).","mechanism":"Ethanolic extract altered tolbutamide (CYP2C9 probe) kinetics in rats; organic extracts inhibited CYP2C9 in vitro, aqueous extract did not.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29042257/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27761064/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp1a2_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP1A2.","mechanism":"Increased phenacetin (CYP1A2 probe) exposure about 1.3-1.4-fold in rats; CYP1A2 inhibition in vitro.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29042257/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27761064/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the risk of liver injury.","mechanism":"Rare reports of Gymnema-associated hepatitis; additive risk theoretical.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK610217/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20856101/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"mulethi","name":"Liquorice / Licorice root","nameHi":"मुलेठी","sanskrit":"Yashtimadhu (यष्टिमधु)","latin":"Glycyrrhiza glabra L.","otherNames":["Jethimadh","Licorice"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for the general wellbeing of the throat, voice and digestion.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Mulethi powder / sticks","Yashtimadhu churna","Licorice tea","Cough lozenges and syrups","DGL (deglycyrrhizinated licorice) tablets"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"hypotension","note":"Regular intake of glycyrrhizin-containing licorice can raise blood pressure, lower potassium, cause fluid retention and heart-rhythm problems (pseudo-aldosteronism). EMA advises against use with high blood pressure, kidney, liver or heart disease, or low potassium.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-glycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34604277/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36843745/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"EMA does not recommend use in pregnancy or breastfeeding; heavy licorice intake in pregnancy has been linked to earlier delivery.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-glycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/licorice-root","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12396997/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Deglycyrrhizinated licorice (DGL) lacks most glycyrrhizin and is less likely to cause these effects.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/licorice-root"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"diuretic_loop_thiazide","severity":"major","effect":"Can cause dangerously low potassium when combined with water tablets.","mechanism":"Glycyrrhizin causes potassium loss and salt/water retention (pseudo-aldosteronism). EMA: not to be used with diuretics.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid this combination unless your doctor agrees. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-glycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1563455/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"major","effect":"Low potassium from licorice can make digoxin toxic (dangerous heart rhythms).","mechanism":"Glycyrrhizin causes potassium loss and salt/water retention (pseudo-aldosteronism). EMA: not to be used with cardiac glycosides.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid this combination unless your doctor agrees. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-glycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiarrhythmic","severity":"major","effect":"Low potassium can trigger dangerous heart rhythms (torsades de pointes) with rhythm medicines.","mechanism":"Glycyrrhizin causes potassium loss and salt/water retention (pseudo-aldosteronism). Torsades de pointes reported with licorice-induced hypokalaemia; EMA warns against use with medicines that aggravate electrolyte imbalance.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Avoid this combination unless your doctor agrees. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-glycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36843745/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"corticosteroid","severity":"major","effect":"Adds to potassium loss and fluid retention, and may increase steroid effects.","mechanism":"Glycyrrhizin inhibits 11β-HSD2 and raises prednisolone levels; EMA: not to be used with corticosteroids.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid this combination unless your doctor agrees. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-glycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/licorice-root","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1752235/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"laxative_stimulant","severity":"major","effect":"Combined potassium loss can become dangerous.","mechanism":"Glycyrrhizin causes potassium loss and salt/water retention (pseudo-aldosteronism). EMA: not to be used with stimulant laxatives.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid this combination unless your doctor agrees. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-glycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-senna-alexandrina-mill-cassia-senna-l-cassia-angustifolia-vahl-folium-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood pressure and work against blood-pressure medicines.","mechanism":"Mineralocorticoid-like effect; EMA states it may counteract antihypertensives.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Have your blood pressure checked.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-glycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP3A4.","mechanism":"Glycyrrhizin 300 mg/day for 14 days modestly induced CYP3A (midazolam AUC down ~23%); EMA advises caution.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-glycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20393696/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hormonal_contraceptive_estrogen","severity":"moderate","effect":"Women on the pill may be more sensitive to licorice side effects (high blood pressure, low potassium, swelling).","mechanism":"In a dose-ranging study, a volunteer taking oral contraceptives developed hypertension, hypokalaemia and oedema on high-dose licorice.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8072387/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower warfarin levels and weaken its effect.","mechanism":"Licorice (Glycyrrhiza uralensis) activated PXR and increased warfarin metabolism in rats; not confirmed in people.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Your INR may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16267138/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"potassium_sparing","severity":"minor","effect":"Licorice's potassium-lowering effect can confuse blood-test results and blood-pressure control in people on these medicines.","mechanism":"Mineralocorticoid-like action opposes spironolactone/eplerenone; theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34604277/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"senna","name":"Senna","nameHi":"सनाय / सोनामुखी","sanskrit":"Svarnapatri (स्वर्णपत्री)","latin":"Senna alexandrina Mill. (syn. Cassia angustifolia Vahl)","otherNames":["Indian senna","Tinnevelly senna"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and Unani for short-term support of bowel regularity.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Senna leaf powder / tea","Senna tablets (sennosides)","Panchsakar churna","Many 'detox' and 'slimming' teas","Pet saffa / laxative churnas"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"EMA contraindicates senna in pregnancy and breastfeeding (genotoxicity concerns with anthranoids; metabolites pass into milk).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-senna-alexandrina-mill-cassia-senna-l-cassia-angustifolia-vahl-folium-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"children","note":"EMA contraindicates use in children under 12.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-senna-alexandrina-mill-cassia-senna-l-cassia-angustifolia-vahl-folium-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"kidney","note":"Long-term use can cause potassium and fluid loss; people with kidney problems should be aware of electrolyte imbalance.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-senna-alexandrina-mill-cassia-senna-l-cassia-angustifolia-vahl-folium-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Rare liver injury has been reported, mostly with long-term high-dose use.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547922/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15956233/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Not for long-term use: prolonged use can cause laxative dependence and bowel dysfunction.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-senna-alexandrina-mill-cassia-senna-l-cassia-angustifolia-vahl-folium-revision-1_en.pdf"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"moderate","effect":"Potassium loss from prolonged use can make digoxin more toxic.","mechanism":"Hypokalaemia from long-term laxative use potentiates cardiac glycosides (EMA).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-senna-alexandrina-mill-cassia-senna-l-cassia-angustifolia-vahl-folium-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiarrhythmic","severity":"moderate","effect":"Potassium loss can increase the risk of heart-rhythm problems with rhythm medicines.","mechanism":"Hypokalaemia interacts with antiarrhythmics and QT-prolonging drugs (EMA).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-senna-alexandrina-mill-cassia-senna-l-cassia-angustifolia-vahl-folium-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"diuretic_loop_thiazide","severity":"moderate","effect":"Adds to potassium loss.","mechanism":"EMA: concomitant diuretics may enhance potassium loss.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-senna-alexandrina-mill-cassia-senna-l-cassia-angustifolia-vahl-folium-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"corticosteroid","severity":"moderate","effect":"Adds to potassium loss.","mechanism":"EMA: concomitant corticosteroids may enhance potassium loss.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-senna-alexandrina-mill-cassia-senna-l-cassia-angustifolia-vahl-folium-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"aloe_vera_oral","name":"Aloe vera (taken by mouth)","nameHi":"घृतकुमारी / ग्वारपाठा","sanskrit":"Kumari (कुमारी)","latin":"Aloe vera (L.) Burm.f. (syn. Aloe barbadensis Mill.)","otherNames":["Aloe juice","Aloe latex"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, as Kumari preparations, for the general wellbeing of digestion and skin.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Aloe vera juice","Aloe-amla juice","Kumaryasava","Aloe latex / 'aloe bitters' laxatives","Aloe vera capsules"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Oral aloe (gel, latex or whole leaf) may be unsafe in pregnancy and breastfeeding; EMA contraindicates aloe latex laxatives in pregnancy, breastfeeding and children under 12.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/aloe-vera","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-aloe-barbadensis-mill-and-aloe-various-species-mainly-aloe-ferox-mill-and-its-hybrids-folii-succus-siccatus_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"other","note":"In 2002 the FDA removed aloe from US over-the-counter laxatives because safety data were lacking; non-decolorised whole-leaf extract caused intestinal tumours in rats (NTP).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2002/05/09/02-11510/status-of-certain-additional-over-the-counter-drug-category-ii-and-iii-active-ingredients","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22968693/"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Rare cases of hepatitis have been reported with oral aloe products.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548634/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17726067/"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Oral aloe may lower blood glucose.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/aloe-vera"]},{"flag":"kidney","note":"Long-term laxative use of aloe latex can cause potassium and fluid loss.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-aloe-barbadensis-mill-and-aloe-various-species-mainly-aloe-ferox-mill-and-its-hybrids-folii-succus-siccatus_en.pdf"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"moderate","effect":"Potassium loss from aloe latex laxatives can make digoxin more toxic.","mechanism":"Hypokalaemia from laxative use potentiates cardiac glycosides (EMA).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-aloe-barbadensis-mill-and-aloe-various-species-mainly-aloe-ferox-mill-and-its-hybrids-folii-succus-siccatus_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiarrhythmic","severity":"moderate","effect":"Potassium loss can increase the risk of heart-rhythm problems.","mechanism":"Hypokalaemia interacts with antiarrhythmics and QT-prolonging drugs (EMA).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-aloe-barbadensis-mill-and-aloe-various-species-mainly-aloe-ferox-mill-and-its-hybrids-folii-succus-siccatus_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"diuretic_loop_thiazide","severity":"moderate","effect":"Adds to potassium loss.","mechanism":"EMA: diuretics may enhance potassium loss with aloe laxatives.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-aloe-barbadensis-mill-and-aloe-various-species-mainly-aloe-ferox-mill-and-its-hybrids-folii-succus-siccatus_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"corticosteroid","severity":"moderate","effect":"Adds to potassium loss.","mechanism":"EMA: corticosteroids may enhance potassium loss with aloe laxatives.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-aloe-barbadensis-mill-and-aloe-various-species-mainly-aloe-ferox-mill-and-its-hybrids-folii-succus-siccatus_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Oral aloe may reduce blood glucose and HbA1c (NCCIH).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/aloe-vera"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Oral aloe may reduce blood glucose and HbA1c (NCCIH).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/aloe-vera"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Oral aloe may reduce blood glucose and HbA1c (NCCIH).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/aloe-vera"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anaesthesia_surgery","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to bleeding during surgery.","mechanism":"Case of excess intraoperative bleeding attributed to aloe plus sevoflurane (both inhibit platelet thromboxane).","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Tell your surgeon, anaesthetist and dentist that you take it. It is commonly advised to stop the herb about 2 weeks before planned surgery; ask your doctor.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15292490/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower thyroid hormone levels.","mechanism":"Leaf extract reduced T3 and T4 in mice.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12065164/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"isabgol","name":"Psyllium husk / Ispaghula","nameHi":"ईसबगोल","sanskrit":"Snigdhajiraka (स्निग्धजीरक)","latin":"Plantago ovata Forssk.","otherNames":["Ispaghula","Psyllium"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and Unani as a dietary fibre for the general wellbeing of bowel regularity.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Isabgol husk (e.g. Sat Isabgol)","Psyllium powder drinks (e.g. Metamucil)","Fibre supplements","Isabgol-containing laxative granules"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Must be taken with plenty of water; taken with too little fluid it can block the throat, oesophagus or bowel. EMA contraindicates it with swallowing difficulty or bowel narrowing/obstruction.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6488929/"]},{"flag":"allergy","note":"Serious allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, have been reported (higher risk in people sensitised by handling the powder).","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14700444/"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Lowers post-meal blood glucose; EMA says people with diabetes should use it under medical supervision as treatment may need adjustment.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26561625/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"lithium","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower lithium blood levels.","mechanism":"Reduced lithium absorption; case report of falling lithium levels; EMA lists lithium.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Take other medicines at least 30-60 minutes before or after isabgol/psyllium, with plenty of water. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Lithium levels may need checking.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1968148/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"moderate","effect":"May reduce digoxin absorption.","mechanism":"Delayed enteral absorption (EMA).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Take other medicines at least 30-60 minutes before or after isabgol/psyllium, with plenty of water. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May change warfarin absorption and INR control.","mechanism":"Delayed absorption of coumarin derivatives (EMA).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Take other medicines at least 30-60 minutes before or after isabgol/psyllium, with plenty of water. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Keep your intake steady and check INR if you start or stop.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May reduce carbamazepine absorption.","mechanism":"Delayed enteral absorption of carbamazepine (EMA).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Take other medicines at least 30-60 minutes before or after isabgol/psyllium, with plenty of water. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"iron_mineral_supplement","severity":"moderate","effect":"May reduce absorption of minerals and vitamin B12.","mechanism":"Delayed absorption of minerals and vitamins (EMA).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Take other medicines at least 30-60 minutes before or after isabgol/psyllium, with plenty of water. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"minor","effect":"May affect levothyroxine absorption.","mechanism":"EMA advises medical supervision; a small human study found only a non-significant reduction in absorption.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Take other medicines at least 30-60 minutes before or after isabgol/psyllium, with plenty of water. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9737361/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower post-meal blood sugar; diabetes treatment may need adjusting.","mechanism":"Soluble fibre slows carbohydrate absorption; EMA advises medical supervision.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Take other medicines at least 30-60 minutes before or after isabgol/psyllium, with plenty of water. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Check your blood sugar more often when starting.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26561625/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower post-meal blood sugar; diabetes treatment may need adjusting.","mechanism":"Soluble fibre slows carbohydrate absorption; EMA advises medical supervision.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Take other medicines at least 30-60 minutes before or after isabgol/psyllium, with plenty of water. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Check your blood sugar more often when starting.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26561625/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower post-meal blood sugar; diabetes treatment may need adjusting.","mechanism":"Soluble fibre slows carbohydrate absorption; EMA advises medical supervision.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Take other medicines at least 30-60 minutes before or after isabgol/psyllium, with plenty of water. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Check your blood sugar more often when starting.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26561625/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"opioid","severity":"moderate","effect":"Higher risk of bowel blockage with medicines that slow the gut.","mechanism":"EMA: use with peristalsis-inhibiting medicines (e.g. opioids) only under medical supervision.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Drink plenty of fluid.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[{"severity":"moderate","effect":"Can delay or reduce absorption of other medicines taken at the same time.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-plantago-ovata-forssk-seminis-tegumentum_en.pdf"]}]},{"id":"shankhpushpi","name":"Shankhpushpi","nameHi":"शंखपुष्पी","sanskrit":"Shankhapushpi (शङ्खपुष्पी)","latin":"Convolvulus pluricaulis Choisy (syn. Convolvulus prostratus Forssk.)","otherNames":["Evolvulus alsinoides and Clitoria ternatea are also sold under this name"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda as a medhya herb for the general wellbeing of memory and calm.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Shankhpushpi syrup","Shankhpushpi churna","Memory/brain tonics with brahmi and shankhpushpi"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"thyroid","note":"Root extract lowered T3 in mice (including mice given levothyroxine).","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11280709/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Several different plants are sold as shankhpushpi; product identity varies.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25182446/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"major","effect":"Has been linked to loss of seizure control with phenytoin.","mechanism":"Two patients on phenytoin taking a Shankhapushpi preparation had lower phenytoin levels and seizures; in rats, repeated dosing lowered phenytoin levels and its anti-seizure effect. Authors advised against the combination.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Avoid this combination unless your neurologist agrees. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1548901/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1484013/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":["fosphenytoin","phenytoin"]},{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"minor","effect":"May reduce the effect of levothyroxine.","mechanism":"Inhibited T3 production (5'-deiodinase) in levothyroxine-treated mice.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11280709/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"punarnava","name":"Punarnava / Spreading hogweed","nameHi":"पुनर्नवा","sanskrit":"Punarnava (पुनर्नवा)","latin":"Boerhavia diffusa L.","otherNames":["Red hogweed","Gadahpurna"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for the general wellbeing of the urinary system and fluid balance.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Punarnava churna","Punarnavadi mandur","Punarnavasava","Punarnava capsules"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Leaf extract lowered blood glucose in diabetic rats.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15036478/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Few human safety data; traditionally regarded as diuretic.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26844923/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"diuretic_loop_thiazide","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the effect of water tablets.","mechanism":"Traditional diuretic use and diuretic activity reported in animal studies; theoretical in people.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26844923/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1178789/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"lithium","severity":"minor","effect":"Diuretic herbs may change lithium levels.","mechanism":"Theoretical: altered fluid/sodium balance can affect lithium clearance.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Lithium levels may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26844923/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Hypoglycaemic effect in diabetic rats.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15036478/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15671692/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Hypoglycaemic effect in diabetic rats.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15036478/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15671692/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Hypoglycaemic effect in diabetic rats.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15036478/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15671692/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"gokshura","name":"Puncture vine / Tribulus","nameHi":"गोखरू","sanskrit":"Gokshura (गोक्षुर)","latin":"Tribulus terrestris L.","otherNames":["Tribulus","Caltrop"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for the general wellbeing of the urinary and male reproductive systems.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Gokshura churna","Gokshuradi guggulu","Tribulus capsules (sports/libido supplements)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"kidney","note":"Severe kidney injury (with raised liver enzymes and neurotoxicity) has been reported after drinking Tribulus water.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20667992/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38328764/"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Rare cholestatic liver injury reported, often with kidney injury.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK583201/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38328764/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Gynaecomastia reported in a young man using a Tribulus product.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15454201/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"nephrotoxic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to kidney stress from medicines that can harm the kidneys.","mechanism":"Tribulus-associated kidney injury case reports; additive risk theoretical.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20667992/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38328764/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the risk of liver injury.","mechanism":"Rare Tribulus-associated liver injury; theoretical additive risk.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK583201/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood pressure further.","mechanism":"ACE-inhibiting and blood-pressure-lowering effect in hypertensive rats.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14519445/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Lowered fasting glucose in a small randomised trial in women with diabetes.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27255456/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Lowered fasting glucose in a small randomised trial in women with diabetes.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27255456/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Lowered fasting glucose in a small randomised trial in women with diabetes.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27255456/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"lithium","severity":"minor","effect":"Diuretic herbs may change lithium levels.","mechanism":"Theoretical: traditional diuretic use may alter lithium clearance.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Lithium levels may need checking.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK583201/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_ssri_snri","severity":"minor","effect":"Isolated side effects (itching, breast milk secretion) were reported with citalopram/escitalopram.","mechanism":"Pharmacovigilance chart review; mechanism unclear.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37829299/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"safed_musli","name":"Safed musli","nameHi":"सफ़ेद मूसली","sanskrit":"Shveta musali (श्वेत मुसली)","latin":"Chlorophytum borivilianum Santapau & R.R.Fern.","otherNames":["White musli"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda as a vajikarana rasayana for general vitality and the wellbeing of the male reproductive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Safed musli powder","Safed musli capsules","Musli pak","Vitality/men's wellness blends"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Root extract lowered blood glucose in diabetic rats.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25249786/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Very few human safety data.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24045177/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in diabetic rats; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25249786/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in diabetic rats; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25249786/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in diabetic rats; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25249786/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"shilajit","name":"Shilajit / Mineral pitch","nameHi":"शिलाजीत","sanskrit":"Shilajatu (शिलाजतु)","latin":"Asphaltum punjabianum (mineral exudate, not a plant)","otherNames":["Mumie","Mumijo","Moomiyo"],"kind":"mineral_preparation","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, after purification (shodhana), as a rasayana for general vitality.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Shilajit resin","Shilajit capsules/tablets","Shuddha shilajit","Shilajit in men's vitality products"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"heavy_metals","note":"Raw or poorly purified shilajit can contain toxic metals; Ayurvedic products bought online have repeatedly tested positive for lead, mercury or arsenic.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34800280/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18728265/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15598918/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Human safety data are limited; it can raise testosterone levels.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23733436/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26395129/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"nephrotoxic","severity":"minor","effect":"Contaminated products could add to kidney stress.","mechanism":"Theoretical: heavy-metal contamination of some products.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Choose only tested, purified products.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34800280/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18728265/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"minor","effect":"Contaminated products could add to liver stress.","mechanism":"Theoretical: heavy-metal contamination of some products.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Choose only tested, purified products.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34800280/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15598918/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"kalonji","name":"Black seed / Black cumin","nameHi":"कलौंजी","sanskrit":"Upakunchika (उपकुञ्चिका), Krishna jiraka","latin":"Nigella sativa L.","otherNames":["Black seed oil","Nigella","Habbatus sauda"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, Unani and Indian cooking for the general wellbeing of digestion.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Kalonji seeds (culinary)","Black seed oil","Black seed capsules","Kalonji honey blends"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Lowered blood glucose in people with type 2 diabetes in clinical trials.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21675032/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25706772/"]},{"flag":"hypotension","note":"Modestly lowers blood pressure in trials.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27512971/"]},{"flag":"kidney","note":"Acute kidney failure was reported in a person taking large amounts of black seed.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23464648/"]},{"flag":"bleeding","note":"Severe low platelets were reported in a patient taking black seed oil with evening primrose oil.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32637272/"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Not linked to clinically apparent liver injury in LiverTox.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591552/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in clinical trials.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21675032/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25706772/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in clinical trials.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21675032/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25706772/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood sugar further with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effect in clinical trials.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21675032/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25706772/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood pressure further.","mechanism":"Blood-pressure-lowering effect in meta-analysis of trials.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27512971/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2d6_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP2D6.","mechanism":"Black seed inhibited CYP2D6 (dextromethorphan O-demethylation) in vitro and in 4 healthy volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20201775/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP3A4.","mechanism":"Black seed inhibited CYP3A4 (dextromethorphan N-demethylation) in vitro and in a small volunteer study.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20201775/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower cyclosporine levels and weaken its effect.","mechanism":"Nigella sativa reduced cyclosporine Cmax by ~36% and AUC by ~56% in rabbits.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Cyclosporine levels may need extra monitoring.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23957013/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise warfarin levels.","mechanism":"Thymoquinone competitively inhibited warfarin 7-hydroxylation (CYP2C9) in vitro; modelling suggested risk above ~1 g/day of seed or oil.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for unusual bruising or bleeding. Tell your surgeon or dentist you take it; it is commonly advised to stop the herbal product about 2 weeks before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35921950/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines broken down by CYP2C9.","mechanism":"Thymoquinone inhibited CYP2C9 in vitro.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35921950/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"nitrate_pde5","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower sildenafil levels.","mechanism":"Reduced sildenafil AUC and Cmax in dogs.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24719213/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"nephrotoxic","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to kidney stress.","mechanism":"Single case of acute kidney failure; theoretical additive risk.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23464648/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"cinnamon","name":"Cinnamon (Ceylon and cassia)","nameHi":"दालचीनी","sanskrit":"Tvak (त्वक्)","latin":"Cinnamomum verum J.Presl (Ceylon); Cinnamomum cassia (L.) J.Presl (cassia)","otherNames":["Cassia","Ceylon cinnamon"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and Indian cooking for the general wellbeing of digestion.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Dalchini sticks / powder (culinary)","Cinnamon capsules","Cinnamon tea","Sitopaladi churna (contains tvak)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Cassia cinnamon contains coumarin, which can harm the liver in sensitive people with prolonged high intake; Ceylon cinnamon contains far less.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/cinnamon","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK623885/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20024932/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Food amounts are likely safe, but larger amounts (supplements) are considered unsafe in pregnancy; EMA does not recommend medicinal use in pregnancy or breastfeeding.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/cinnamon","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/community-herbal-monograph-cinnamomum-verum-js-presl-cortex_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"allergy","note":"EMA contraindicates use with allergy to cinnamon or Peru balsam.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/community-herbal-monograph-cinnamomum-verum-js-presl-cortex_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Cinnamon supplements may modestly lower fasting glucose.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24019277/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"moderate","effect":"Cassia cinnamon supplements may add to liver stress with medicines that can harm the liver.","mechanism":"Coumarin hepatotoxicity in sensitive individuals; additive risk theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you take supplements, Ceylon cinnamon contains much less coumarin.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/cinnamon","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20024932/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27378929/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Modest fasting-glucose lowering in meta-analysis.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24019277/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Modest fasting-glucose lowering in meta-analysis.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24019277/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May lower blood sugar slightly with diabetes medicines.","mechanism":"Modest fasting-glucose lowering in meta-analysis.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24019277/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticancer","severity":"minor","effect":"Possible interaction with letrozole was predicted, but a clinical study found none.","mechanism":"Cinnamaldehyde inactivates CYP2A6 in vitro (NCCIH notes theoretical concern); a 2026 clinical study of Ceylon cinnamon found no change in letrozole or nicotine levels.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/cinnamon","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26851241/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41622703/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"haritaki","name":"Chebulic myrobalan","nameHi":"हरड़ / हरीतकी","sanskrit":"Haritaki","latin":"Terminalia chebula Retz.","otherNames":["Harada","Kadukkai (Tamil)"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive system and as a rasayana (rejuvenative) herb.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Haritaki churna","Triphala churna (with amla and bibhitaki)","Abhayarishta","Haritaki capsules"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Has a laxative action at normal doses and is described as purgative at high doses; may cause loose stools or cramping, which can matter for people prone to dehydration.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28696777/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"laxative_stimulant","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the effect of other laxatives, causing loose stools or cramping.","mechanism":"Additive laxative effect (Triphala/haritaki described as laxative at normal doses, purgative at high doses).","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28696777/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory alter levels of CYP3A4-metabolised medicines.","mechanism":"Triphala (containing haritaki) weakly inhibited CYP3A4 in vitro; in contrast, chebulinic acid induced several CYP enzymes in rats. Direction and clinical relevance unknown.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20883765/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39683707/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2d6_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory alter levels of CYP2D6-metabolised medicines.","mechanism":"Triphala weakly inhibited CYP2D6 in vitro (high IC50 values); no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20883765/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"ppi_antacid","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory raise levels of omeprazole-type medicines.","mechanism":"In rats, 15 days of T. chebula increased omeprazole (CYP2C19 probe) and chlorzoxazone (CYP2E1 probe) exposure; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33425113/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"bibhitaki","name":"Belleric myrobalan","nameHi":"बहेड़ा","sanskrit":"Bibhitaki","latin":"Terminalia bellirica (Gaertn.) Roxb.","otherNames":["Bahera","Vibhitaki"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the respiratory and digestive systems, most often as one of the three fruits of Triphala.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Triphala churna","Bibhitaki churna","Triphala guggulu"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Mainly consumed within Triphala, which is laxative at normal doses; may cause loose stools.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28696777/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"laxative_stimulant","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the effect of other laxatives when taken as Triphala.","mechanism":"Additive laxative effect of Triphala.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28696777/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory alter levels of CYP3A4-metabolised medicines.","mechanism":"Individual Triphala components, including T. bellirica, inhibited rat liver CYP activity in vitro; Triphala inhibited CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 weakly. No human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20883765/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32781393/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"kutki","name":"Kutki / Picrorhiza","nameHi":"कुटकी","sanskrit":"Katuka / Katurohini","latin":"Picrorhiza kurroa Royle ex Benth.","otherNames":["Kadu"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the liver and digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Kutki churna","Kutki capsules","Arogyavardhini vati (contains kutki)","Picroliv (standardised fraction)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Very bitter herb with limited human safety data; standardised extracts (picroliv) are biologically active on the immune system in animal studies.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1484891/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36500409/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory work against immune-suppressing medicines.","mechanism":"Picroliv (iridoid glycoside fraction) stimulated antibody and cell-mediated immune responses in mice; no human interaction data.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Especially relevant after an organ transplant.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1484891/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory alter levels of CYP2C-metabolised medicines.","mechanism":"Picroside II (a kutki constituent) did not inhibit human liver microsomal CYPs in vitro, but repeated dosing inhibited CYP2C activity in rats.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37192720/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory alter levels of CYP3A4-metabolised medicines.","mechanism":"Low-dose picroside II increased CYP3A activity in rats; human relevance unknown.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37192720/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"kalmegh","name":"Andrographis / Green chiretta","nameHi":"कालमेघ","sanskrit":"Kalamegha / Bhunimba","latin":"Andrographis paniculata (Burm.f.) Nees","otherNames":["King of bitters","Chuan Xin Lian"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the liver, digestion and seasonal immunity.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Kalmegh churna/capsules","Andrographis extract tablets","Kan Jang (with Eleutherococcus)","Liver and immunity blends"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"allergy","note":"Australia's regulator (TGA) has received hundreds of reports of hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis with Andrographis products, including a fatal case, and reactions occurred in people who had used it before without problems. In 2026 the TGA removed it from ingredients permitted in new listed medicines.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.tga.gov.au/safety/safety-monitoring-and-information/safety-alerts/products-containing-andrographis-paniculata","https://www.tga.gov.au/safety/safety-monitoring-and-information/safety-alerts/medicines-containing-andrographis-paniculata-safety-advisory"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Not recommended in pregnancy: animal studies report anti-fertility and pregnancy-terminating effects; EMA HMPC could not establish a monograph because of insufficient data.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-report/draft-assessment-report-andrographis-paniculata-nees-folium-first-version_en.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/public-statement/final-public-statement-andrographis-paniculata-nees-folium-first-version_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"kidney","note":"Acute kidney injury has been reported in China with intravenous andrographolide preparations (not oral herb); relevance to oral use is uncertain.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24148088/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Common non-serious side effects in trials are gastrointestinal and skin reactions.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33372366/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase warfarin effect and bleeding risk.","mechanism":"Andrographolide increased warfarin exposure in rats and Andrographis constituents inhibit platelet aggregation in vitro; a rat study with the Kan Jang combination found no significant warfarin effect, so data conflict.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Check INR more often if you start or stop the herbal product.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29983086/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17081514/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-report/draft-assessment-report-andrographis-paniculata-nees-folium-first-version_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-thinning effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Andrographolide and 14-deoxy-11,12-didehydroandrographolide inhibit human platelet aggregation in vitro.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17081514/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10228608/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anaesthesia_surgery","severity":"minor","effect":"Possible added bleeding tendency around surgery.","mechanism":"Theoretical, based on in vitro antiplatelet activity.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Tell your surgeon and anaesthetist about this herb; ask your doctor whether to pause the herbal product before planned surgery.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17081514/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to blood-pressure-lowering medicines.","mechanism":"Hypotensive effect of extracts and diterpenoids in rats; no human interaction data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8886488/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17650544/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase glibenclamide (glyburide) levels and the risk of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Andrographolide increased glyburide bioavailability and hypoglycaemic effect in diabetic rats, attributed to CYP3A4 inhibition.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26762235/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise levels of CYP3A4-metabolised medicines.","mechanism":"Extracts inhibit CYP3A4 in human liver microsomes and reduce CYP3A expression in human hepatocytes (summarised by EMA HMPC).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-report/draft-assessment-report-andrographis-paniculata-nees-folium-first-version_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise levels of CYP2C9-metabolised medicines (e.g. warfarin, some sulfonylureas).","mechanism":"Extracts inhibit CYP2C9 (tolbutamide hydroxylation) in human liver microsomes in vitro (summarised by EMA HMPC).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-report/draft-assessment-report-andrographis-paniculata-nees-folium-first-version_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"theophylline","severity":"minor","effect":"May change theophylline levels (direction unclear).","mechanism":"In rats, andrographolide increased theophylline clearance, while whole extract slowed elimination at higher theophylline doses; andrographolide induces CYP1A in mouse hepatocytes.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20096675/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-report/draft-assessment-report-andrographis-paniculata-nees-folium-first-version_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory work against immune-suppressing medicines.","mechanism":"Immunostimulant activity of andrographolide and extracts in experimental studies; no human interaction data.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-report/draft-assessment-report-andrographis-paniculata-nees-folium-first-version_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"moringa","name":"Moringa / Drumstick tree","nameHi":"सहजन","sanskrit":"Shigru","latin":"Moringa oleifera Lam.","otherNames":["Murungai (Tamil)","Malunggay"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and as a food for general nutritional wellbeing.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Moringa leaf powder","Moringa capsules","Moringa tea","Drumstick (food)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Generally well tolerated and not linked to liver enzyme rises, but implicated in one case of acute anicteric hepatitis.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK605172/"]},{"flag":"breastfeeding","note":"Used as a galactagogue; no serious short-term effects seen in nursing mothers or infants, but long-term infant data are lacking.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK501899/"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Leaf powder lowered blood glucose in small human studies.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31810205/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25808883/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to glucose-lowering medicines.","mechanism":"Glucose-lowering effects of leaf preparations in small human and animal studies.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31810205/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35010932/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to glucose-lowering effect, with some risk of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Additive pharmacodynamic effect inferred from human glucose-lowering data.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31810205/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to insulin's glucose-lowering effect.","mechanism":"Additive pharmacodynamic effect inferred from human glucose-lowering data.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31810205/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May add slightly to blood-pressure-lowering medicines.","mechanism":"Moringa leaves lowered postprandial blood pressure in a small human study.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31063434/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory alter thyroid hormone balance.","mechanism":"Leaf extract lowered T3 and raised T4 in rats, suggesting reduced T4-to-T3 conversion; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10675284/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiretroviral","severity":"minor","effect":"Unlikely to change nevirapine levels at food-like doses.","mechanism":"Moringa inhibits CYP3A4/1A2/2D6 in vitro, but 1.85 g/day leaf powder for 14 days did not meaningfully change nevirapine pharmacokinetics in HIV patients.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28293270/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory raise levels of CYP3A4-metabolised medicines at high extract doses.","mechanism":"Leaf extracts and isolated constituents inhibit CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 in vitro; a human nevirapine study showed no meaningful effect.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31301970/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28293270/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2d6_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory raise levels of CYP2D6-metabolised medicines at high extract doses.","mechanism":"Leaf extracts inhibit CYP2D6 in vitro; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31301970/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory reduce the effect of anticoagulants.","mechanism":"LactMed notes that moringa may stimulate blood clotting and advises caution in people at high risk of clots; mechanism not established.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK501899/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"ajwain","name":"Ajwain / Carom seed","nameHi":"अजवाइन","sanskrit":"Yavani","latin":"Trachyspermum ammi (L.) Sprague","otherNames":["Carum copticum","Omum"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and Indian cooking for general wellbeing of the digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Ajwain seeds (spice)","Ajwain arka / omum water","Digestive churnas containing yavani"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Seeds have been reported as used to induce abortion in a 1987 Indian survey; avoid medicinal (non-culinary) doses in pregnancy.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22654405/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"Concentrated extracts may add to the effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Ethereal extract inhibited human platelet aggregation in vitro via reduced thromboxane formation; culinary amounts unlikely to matter.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3141935/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"Concentrated extracts could in theory add to bleeding risk with warfarin.","mechanism":"Theoretical, based on in vitro antiplatelet activity.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3141935/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"Concentrated extracts may add to blood-pressure-lowering medicines.","mechanism":"Seed extract lowered blood pressure in rats with calcium-channel-blocking activity in isolated tissues.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15763373/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"black_pepper","name":"Black pepper (piperine)","nameHi":"काली मिर्च","sanskrit":"Maricha","latin":"Piper nigrum L.","otherNames":["Piperine","BioPerine (piperine extract)"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and cooking for general wellbeing of digestion, and as an ingredient that enhances the uptake of other herbs.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Black pepper (spice)","Piperine / BioPerine capsules","Curcumin + piperine supplements","Trikatu churna (with pippali and ginger)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Piperine is a 'bioavailability enhancer': it can raise blood levels of many medicines and of other supplements (for example, curcumin levels rose about 20-fold with 20 mg piperine).","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9619120/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29250980/"]},{"flag":"breastfeeding","note":"Dietary pepper is compatible with breastfeeding; no studies of piperine supplements in nursing mothers.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK579035/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of phenytoin and carbamazepine, increasing the risk of side effects such as dizziness or unsteadiness.","mechanism":"Piperine increased phenytoin and carbamazepine exposure (AUC) in human studies, including patients with epilepsy; thought to reflect reduced metabolism and increased absorption.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Normal culinary use is unlikely to matter; concentrated extracts and 'bioavailability-enhancer' capsules are the concern.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3444866/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16767797/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19283724/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27776366/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"theophylline","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase theophylline levels and prolong its half-life.","mechanism":"Piperine 20 mg/day for 7 days raised theophylline Cmax, AUC and half-life in healthy volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Normal culinary use is unlikely to matter; concentrated extracts and 'bioavailability-enhancer' capsules are the concern.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1815977/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp1a2_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"May slow clearance of some CYP1A2-metabolised medicines (theophylline is the best-studied example).","mechanism":"Inferred from the human theophylline study (theophylline is mainly cleared by CYP1A2); direct CYP1A2 data are limited.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1815977/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May increase blood levels of propranolol.","mechanism":"Piperine gave an earlier and higher propranolol peak and higher AUC in healthy volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor pulse and blood pressure if starting a piperine supplement.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1815977/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines cleared by CYP3A4 (e.g. cyclosporine, tacrolimus, some antiepileptics).","mechanism":"Piperine inhibits human CYP3A4 in vitro; however it also activates PXR and can induce CYP3A4/MDR1 expression in human cells, so the net clinical effect may vary with dose and duration.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12130727/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23707768/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29250980/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"pgp_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase absorption of P-glycoprotein substrate drugs such as digoxin or dabigatran.","mechanism":"Piperine inhibits human P-gp in vitro and 20 mg/day for 10 days increased fexofenadine (a P-gp probe) Cmax and AUC in healthy volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Extra caution with narrow-margin P-gp substrates such as digoxin.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12130727/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27981349/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise digoxin levels.","mechanism":"Theoretical extrapolation: digoxin is a P-gp substrate and piperine inhibits P-gp in vitro and in a human fexofenadine study; no direct digoxin study found.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12130727/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27981349/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiretroviral","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase blood levels of nevirapine.","mechanism":"Piperine 20 mg/day raised nevirapine Cmax and AUC substantially in a small crossover study in healthy men.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Do not change HIV medicine routines without your HIV clinician.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17963429/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antitubercular","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise rifampicin blood levels.","mechanism":"Piperine increased rifampicin blood levels in patients with tuberculosis; piperine is combined with rifampicin in at least one Indian product (Risorine).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4044481/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29313577/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"pippali","name":"Long pepper","nameHi":"पिप्पली / पीपल","sanskrit":"Pippali","latin":"Piper longum L.","otherNames":["Pippalimool (root)"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the respiratory and digestive systems and as a rasayana.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Pippali churna","Trikatu churna","Pippalyasava","Chyawanprash (contains pippali)","Sitopaladi churna"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Contains piperine, a bioavailability enhancer that can raise blood levels of medicines and other herbs; Trikatu was used historically for this purpose.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16767797/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15120451/"]},{"flag":"breastfeeding","note":"Long pepper is reportedly used in India to increase milk supply, but no valid trials support this and safety data are lacking.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK579035/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of phenytoin and carbamazepine, increasing the risk of side effects such as dizziness or unsteadiness.","mechanism":"Piperine (main alkaloid of black and long pepper) increased phenytoin and carbamazepine exposure (AUC) in human studies, including patients with epilepsy; thought to reflect reduced metabolism and increased absorption. Evidence comes from isolated piperine (about 20 mg/day), not from whole long pepper.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Normal culinary use is unlikely to matter; concentrated extracts and 'bioavailability-enhancer' capsules are the concern.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3444866/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16767797/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19283724/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27776366/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"theophylline","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase theophylline levels and prolong its half-life.","mechanism":"Piperine (main alkaloid of black and long pepper) 20 mg/day for 7 days raised theophylline Cmax, AUC and half-life in healthy volunteers. Evidence comes from isolated piperine (about 20 mg/day), not from whole long pepper.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Normal culinary use is unlikely to matter; concentrated extracts and 'bioavailability-enhancer' capsules are the concern.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1815977/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp1a2_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"May slow clearance of some CYP1A2-metabolised medicines (theophylline is the best-studied example).","mechanism":"Inferred from the human theophylline study (theophylline is mainly cleared by CYP1A2); direct CYP1A2 data are limited. Evidence comes from isolated piperine (about 20 mg/day), not from whole long pepper.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1815977/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May increase blood levels of propranolol.","mechanism":"Piperine (main alkaloid of black and long pepper) gave an earlier and higher propranolol peak and higher AUC in healthy volunteers. Evidence comes from isolated piperine (about 20 mg/day), not from whole long pepper.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor pulse and blood pressure if starting a piperine supplement.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1815977/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood levels of medicines cleared by CYP3A4 (e.g. cyclosporine, tacrolimus, some antiepileptics).","mechanism":"Piperine (main alkaloid of black and long pepper) inhibits human CYP3A4 in vitro; however it also activates PXR and can induce CYP3A4/MDR1 expression in human cells, so the net clinical effect may vary with dose and duration. Evidence comes from isolated piperine (about 20 mg/day), not from whole long pepper.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12130727/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23707768/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29250980/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"pgp_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase absorption of P-glycoprotein substrate drugs such as digoxin or dabigatran.","mechanism":"Piperine (main alkaloid of black and long pepper) inhibits human P-gp in vitro and 20 mg/day for 10 days increased fexofenadine (a P-gp probe) Cmax and AUC in healthy volunteers. Evidence comes from isolated piperine (about 20 mg/day), not from whole long pepper.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Extra caution with narrow-margin P-gp substrates such as digoxin.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12130727/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27981349/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise digoxin levels.","mechanism":"Theoretical extrapolation: digoxin is a P-gp substrate and piperine inhibits P-gp in vitro and in a human fexofenadine study; no direct digoxin study found. Evidence comes from isolated piperine (about 20 mg/day), not from whole long pepper.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12130727/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27981349/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiretroviral","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase blood levels of nevirapine.","mechanism":"Piperine (main alkaloid of black and long pepper) 20 mg/day raised nevirapine Cmax and AUC substantially in a small crossover study in healthy men. Evidence comes from isolated piperine (about 20 mg/day), not from whole long pepper.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Do not change HIV medicine routines without your HIV clinician.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17963429/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antitubercular","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise rifampicin blood levels.","mechanism":"Piperine (main alkaloid of black and long pepper) increased rifampicin blood levels in patients with tuberculosis; piperine is combined with rifampicin in at least one Indian product (Risorine). Evidence comes from isolated piperine (about 20 mg/day), not from whole long pepper.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4044481/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29313577/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"nsaid","severity":"minor","effect":"Trikatu (pippali + black pepper + ginger) may reduce diclofenac levels.","mechanism":"In rabbits, Trikatu pretreatment lowered serum diclofenac and reduced its anti-inflammatory effect, the opposite direction to most piperine data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15120451/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"saffron","name":"Saffron","nameHi":"केसर","sanskrit":"Kumkuma / Kunkuma","latin":"Crocus sativus L.","otherNames":["Zafran"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and cooking for general wellbeing of mood, skin and complexion.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Kesar strands (spice)","Saffron extract capsules (e.g. affron, Satiereal)","Kumkumadi tailam (topical)","Chyawanprash (some brands)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Low culinary doses appear tolerated, but higher doses are linked to uterine stimulation and miscarriage risk; avoid supplement doses in pregnancy.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42186868/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Therapeutic doses (about 30 mg/day of extract) show little toxicity, but high doses (several grams) are toxic.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28293386/"]},{"flag":"hypotension","note":"400 mg/day for a week lowered standing systolic and mean arterial pressure in healthy volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18693099/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"May add slightly to antiplatelet medicines at supplement doses.","mechanism":"Saffron extract inhibits human platelet aggregation in vitro; a human trial found no change in clotting tests at 200-400 mg/day, so clinical relevance is uncertain.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16180089/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23733488/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory add to bleeding risk with warfarin at high doses.","mechanism":"Theoretical (in vitro antiplatelet activity); 200-400 mg/day did not change PT, PTT or coagulation factors in healthy volunteers.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16180089/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23733488/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to blood-pressure-lowering medicines at higher doses.","mechanism":"400 mg/day lowered standing systolic and mean arterial pressure in healthy volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18693099/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_ssri_snri","severity":"minor","effect":"Possible additive effects on mood-related brain chemistry.","mechanism":"Proposed serotonergic mechanism of saffron's antidepressant effect; no interaction studies with SSRIs/SNRIs found.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Do not use saffron supplements in place of prescribed treatment.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25384672/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"vacha","name":"Sweet flag / Calamus","nameHi":"वच","sanskrit":"Vacha","latin":"Acorus calamus L.","otherNames":["Calamus root"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the nervous system, speech and memory.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Vacha churna","Saraswatarishta (contains vacha)","Brahmi/medhya blends with vacha","Calamus oil"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Contains beta-asarone, which is genotoxic and a liver carcinogen in rodents. EMA HMPC recommends limiting beta-asarone exposure from herbal medicines (about 115 micrograms/day) and favouring low-asarone (diploid) varieties; the US FDA prohibits calamus and its oil/extract in food.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/public-statement-use-herbal-medicinal-products-containing-asarone_en.pdf","https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-189/subpart-C/section-189.110"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Because of the genotoxicity concerns above and lack of safety data, avoid in pregnancy and breastfeeding.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/public-statement-use-herbal-medicinal-products-containing-asarone_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"children","note":"Traditionally given to children in some regions; asarone exposure limits are body-weight based, so children are relatively more exposed.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/public-statement-use-herbal-medicinal-products-containing-asarone_en.pdf"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"sedative_hypnotic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase drowsiness with sleeping pills, benzodiazepines or barbiturates.","mechanism":"Calamus oil and asarone potentiated barbiturate sleep in animals; ethanol extract shows CNS-depressant actions in rodents.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Avoid driving if drowsy.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/13932508/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2314110/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"jatamansi","name":"Spikenard / Indian nard","nameHi":"जटामांसी","sanskrit":"Jatamamsi","latin":"Nardostachys jatamansi (D.Don) DC.","otherNames":["Muskroot","Sumbul-ut-teeb (Unani)"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of sleep and the nervous system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Jatamansi churna","Jatamansi capsules","Sleep and relaxation blends","Jatamansi oil"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Classified as hypno-sedative in Ayurveda; may cause drowsiness.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29934858/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"sedative_hypnotic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to drowsiness from sleeping pills, benzodiazepines or other sedatives.","mechanism":"Anxiolytic action in mice was blocked by flumazenil, indicating a GABA-benzodiazepine receptor mechanism; additive sedation is plausible.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Avoid driving if drowsy.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29934858/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to blood-pressure-lowering medicines.","mechanism":"Lowered blood pressure in a small randomised study in people with high blood pressure.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33066862/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"sarpagandha","name":"Indian snakeroot","nameHi":"सर्पगंधा","sanskrit":"Sarpagandha","latin":"Rauvolfia serpentina (L.) Benth. ex Kurz","otherNames":["Rauwolfia","Reserpine (main alkaloid)"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the heart, circulation and calm sleep.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Sarpagandha churna","Sarpagandha vati / ghan vati","Sarpagandhadi churna","BP and sleep blends containing sarpagandha"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Contains reserpine, a prescription-strength drug that depletes noradrenaline, dopamine and serotonin. Reserpine is contraindicated in people with a history of depression and in peptic ulcer or ulcerative colitis; it has long been linked to low mood (a 2023 systematic review found the evidence inconsistent), and can cause nasal congestion and slow heart rate. Use only under medical supervision.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557767/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36000248/"]},{"flag":"hypotension","note":"Lowers blood pressure (reserpine is an established antihypertensive); can cause excessive drops when combined with other BP-lowering medicines.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27997978/","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead"]},{"flag":"surgery","note":"Unexpected hypotension and bradycardia have occurred under anaesthesia in patients taking reserpine; the label advised stopping 1-2 weeks before elective surgery.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead"]},{"flag":"breastfeeding","note":"Reserpine passes into breast milk; LactMed prefers an alternative, especially with newborn or preterm infants.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK501093/","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead"]},{"flag":"other","note":"People taking levodopa should avoid reserpine-containing products: reserpine reduces the response to levodopa (no dedicated drug_class key exists for antiparkinson medicines).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidepressant_maoi","severity":"major","effect":"Excitation and high blood pressure.","mechanism":"Reserpine releases/depletes monoamines; the reserpine label states that adding reserpine in patients on MAO inhibitors can cause excitation and hypertension and the combination should be avoided.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid this combination unless your doctor specifically approves it. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anaesthesia_surgery","severity":"major","effect":"Severe drop in blood pressure and slow heart rate during anaesthesia.","mechanism":"Reduced responsiveness to noradrenaline after catecholamine depletion by reserpine.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Tell your surgeon and anaesthetist about this herb; ask your doctor whether to pause the herbal product before planned surgery. The reserpine label advised stopping 1-2 weeks before elective surgery.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"moderate","effect":"Higher risk of irregular heart rhythm with digoxin.","mechanism":"Reserpine label: concurrent use with digitalis may enhance arrhythmias.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiarrhythmic","severity":"moderate","effect":"Higher risk of irregular heart rhythm (quinidine named on label).","mechanism":"Reserpine label: use cautiously with quinidine because arrhythmias may be enhanced.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"moderate","effect":"Blood pressure may fall too low; beta-blocker effects (slow pulse, tiredness) may be increased.","mechanism":"Additive blood-pressure lowering; reserpine label notes additive CNS-depressant and cardiovascular effects with beta-blockers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood pressure and pulse.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27997978/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"diuretic_loop_thiazide","severity":"moderate","effect":"Blood pressure may fall too low.","mechanism":"Additive antihypertensive effect (reserpine was marketed in fixed combination with thiazides).","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"sedative_hypnotic","severity":"moderate","effect":"Increased drowsiness and slowed reactions.","mechanism":"Reserpine label: additive CNS-depressant effects with barbiturates and alcohol.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Avoid alcohol and driving if drowsy.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_tca","severity":"minor","effect":"May worsen low mood or reduce the benefit of antidepressants.","mechanism":"Reserpine depletes brain monoamines and its label contraindicates use with a history of depression; however, a 2023 systematic review found its association with depression inconsistent. No specific interaction studies found.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36000248/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_ssri_snri","severity":"minor","effect":"May worsen low mood or reduce the benefit of antidepressants.","mechanism":"Monoamine depletion by reserpine; label contraindication in depression; evidence on depression is inconsistent. No specific SSRI/SNRI interaction studies found.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36000248/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiparkinson_levodopa","severity":"moderate","effect":"May reduce how well levodopa works.","mechanism":"Reserpine in sarpagandha depletes dopamine; the levodopa label advises against reserpine.","evidence":"regulatory","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b503fa5e-5726-426d-867c-e8fe59ed3ead","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"kapikacchu","name":"Velvet bean","nameHi":"कौंच / कपिकच्छू","sanskrit":"Kapikacchu / Atmagupta","latin":"Mucuna pruriens (L.) DC.","otherNames":["Cowhage","Mucuna"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the nervous and reproductive systems and vitality.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Kapikacchu churna","Kaunch beej powder","Mucuna extract capsules (often labelled with L-dopa %)","Vajikarana / men's vitality blends"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Seeds naturally contain levodopa (L-dopa), the same active drug used in Parkinson's medicines; supplement levodopa content varies widely and is often not stated accurately. People taking levodopa/carbidopa, other dopaminergic medicines or dopamine-blocking medicines should not use it without their prescriber (no dedicated drug_class key exists for antiparkinson medicines).","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35939305/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15548480/","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66"]},{"flag":"other","note":"An outbreak of acute toxic psychosis was attributed to eating Mucuna beans in Mozambique; levodopa can cause hallucinations and psychotic-like behaviour.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1978001/","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Contains levodopa; levodopa/carbidopa caused malformations in rabbits and there are no adequate human pregnancy data.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66"]},{"flag":"breastfeeding","note":"Levodopa can lower prolactin and may reduce milk supply.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK501337/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidepressant_maoi","severity":"major","effect":"Dangerous rise in blood pressure (hypertensive reaction).","mechanism":"Mucuna seed preparations deliver clinically active levodopa in humans; the FDA-approved levodopa label contraindicates non-selective MAO inhibitors. No study of Mucuna plus MAOIs exists, so severity rests on the regulatory contraindication for levodopa.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Avoid this combination. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15548480/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35939305/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antipsychotic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May reduce the effect of antipsychotics, and antipsychotics may block mucuna's dopamine effect; risk of worsening psychotic symptoms.","mechanism":"Dopamine D2 antagonists reduce levodopa effect; levodopa can cause hallucinations/psychosis.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1978001/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"moderate","effect":"Dizziness or fainting from low blood pressure on standing.","mechanism":"Symptomatic postural hypotension reported when levodopa was added to antihypertensive treatment (levodopa content of Mucuna).","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Rise slowly from sitting or lying.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_tca","severity":"minor","effect":"Rare high blood pressure or abnormal movements.","mechanism":"Rare reports of hypertension and dyskinesia with tricyclics plus levodopa.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"iron_mineral_supplement","severity":"minor","effect":"Iron may reduce absorption of mucuna's levodopa.","mechanism":"Iron salts reduce levodopa availability (levodopa label).","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Separate doses by at least 2 hours and talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antitubercular","severity":"minor","effect":"Isoniazid may reduce the effect of mucuna's levodopa.","mechanism":"Isoniazid can reduce the therapeutic effect of levodopa (levodopa label).","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"minor","effect":"Phenytoin may reduce the effect of mucuna's levodopa.","mechanism":"Phenytoin has been reported to reverse levodopa's effect (levodopa label).","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiparkinson_levodopa","severity":"moderate","effect":"Adds extra levodopa, which may cause unwanted movements, low blood pressure or confusion.","mechanism":"Mucuna seeds naturally contain levodopa, in amounts that vary between products.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35939305/","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9b17b028-964a-473c-823d-81423535bd66"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"bhringraj","name":"False daisy","nameHi":"भृंगराज","sanskrit":"Bhringaraja","latin":"Eclipta prostrata (L.) L.","otherNames":["Eclipta alba"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the liver, hair and skin.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Bhringraj oil (hair)","Bhringraj churna","Bhringrajasava","Liver blends containing bhringraj"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"manjistha","name":"Indian madder","nameHi":"मंजीठ","sanskrit":"Manjishtha","latin":"Rubia cordifolia L.","otherNames":[],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the skin and blood.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Manjistha churna","Manjishthadi kwath","Skin/blood-purifier blends","Manjistha capsules"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"ashoka","name":"Ashoka tree","nameHi":"अशोक","sanskrit":"Ashoka","latin":"Saraca asoca (Roxb.) W.J.de Wilde","otherNames":["Saraca indica"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the female reproductive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Ashokarishta","Ashoka churna","Women's wellness blends (e.g. with lodhra, shatavari)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Commercial ashoka bark is frequently adulterated or substituted with other species (e.g. Polyalthia longifolia), so product content may not match the label.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28808391/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27627901/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Flower extract showed oestrogen-like activity in ovariectomised rats; no human pregnancy safety data.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27884717/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"hormonal_contraceptive_estrogen","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory add to or interfere with oestrogen-containing medicines.","mechanism":"Oestrogenic activity of flower extract in ovariectomised rats; bark (the usual product) not tested in this way; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27884717/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"lodhra","name":"Lodh tree","nameHi":"लोध्र","sanskrit":"Lodhra","latin":"Symplocos racemosa Roxb.","otherNames":[],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the female reproductive system and skin.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Lodhra churna","Lodhrasava","Women's wellness blends","Pushyanug churna (contains lodhra)"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory add to glucose-lowering medicines.","mechanism":"Anti-diabetic activity reported in experimental studies of the stem bark (review); no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26851499/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hormonal_contraceptive_estrogen","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory interfere with hormonal medicines.","mechanism":"Anti-androgenic activity reported in experimental studies (review); clinical relevance unknown.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26851499/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"daruharidra","name":"Indian barberry (berberine)","nameHi":"दारुहल्दी","sanskrit":"Daruharidra","latin":"Berberis aristata DC.","otherNames":["Berberine","Rasanjana / Rasaut (extract)"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the skin, eyes, liver and metabolism.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Daruharidra churna","Rasaut / rasanjana","Berberine HCl capsules (often 500 mg)","Blood-sugar and weight blends with berberine"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Berberine can displace bilirubin from albumin; authorities advise against use in pregnancy, breastfeeding and in infants because of the risk to newborns.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/goldenseal","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK501866/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8513024/"]},{"flag":"breastfeeding","note":"Avoid exposing newborns to berberine via breast milk (bilirubin displacement concern).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK501866/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/goldenseal"]},{"flag":"children","note":"Should not be given to infants/newborns, especially if jaundiced.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/goldenseal","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8513024/"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Lowers blood glucose; a case of severe hypoglycaemia with syncope was reported in a non-diabetic athlete using berberine supplements.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40903097/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36467075/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"major","effect":"Raises cyclosporine blood levels, risking toxicity.","mechanism":"In kidney-transplant recipients, berberine 0.2 g three times daily raised cyclosporine trough levels and AUC (about 35% higher AUC); attributed to CYP3A4 and intestinal P-gp inhibition.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid unless your transplant team approves and monitors levels. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16133554/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19370549/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":["ciclosporin","cyclosporine"]},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise levels of CYP3A4-metabolised medicines (e.g. midazolam, tacrolimus, some statins).","mechanism":"Berberine 300 mg three times daily for 2 weeks increased midazolam AUC about 40% in healthy men.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21870106/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2d6_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise levels of CYP2D6-metabolised medicines (e.g. some antidepressants, beta-blockers, dextromethorphan).","mechanism":"Same human study: dextromethorphan/dextrorphan ratio rose about ninefold, indicating strong CYP2D6 inhibition.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21870106/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise levels of CYP2C9-metabolised medicines (e.g. losartan, warfarin, some sulfonylureas).","mechanism":"Same human study: losartan/E-3174 ratio doubled, indicating reduced CYP2C9 activity.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21870106/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May change warfarin levels and bleeding risk.","mechanism":"Extrapolated from CYP2C9 inhibition in humans; no direct warfarin study found.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Check INR more often if you start or stop berberine.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21870106/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"pgp_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase absorption of P-gp substrate drugs.","mechanism":"Berberine increased oral digoxin and cyclosporine bioavailability in rats via intestinal P-gp inhibition.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19370549/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise digoxin levels.","mechanism":"Dose-dependent increase in oral digoxin AUC/Cmax in rats (P-gp inhibition); no human study found.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Digoxin levels may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19370549/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"Higher risk of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Additive glucose lowering (meta-analysis of trials); berberine also reported to act as an insulin secretagogue.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36467075/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34556670/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"Higher risk of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Additive glucose lowering.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36467075/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"Additive glucose lowering; may slightly reduce metformin absorption at lower metformin doses.","mechanism":"Goldenseal (berberine-containing) reduced metformin AUC by about 20% at low metformin doses but not overall in adults with type 2 diabetes; transporter (OCT/MATE) interactions shown in vitro.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39943692/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30428337/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"vijaysar","name":"Indian kino tree","nameHi":"विजयसार","sanskrit":"Asana / Bijaka","latin":"Pterocarpus marsupium Roxb.","otherNames":["Vijayasar"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of metabolism.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Vijaysar wooden tumbler (water stored overnight)","Vijaysar churna/extract","Blood-sugar blends (e.g. with gurmar, jamun)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Lowered fasting and post-meal glucose in an ICMR multicentre open trial; may cause low blood sugar with glucose-lowering medicines.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9745215/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"Higher risk of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Additive glucose lowering: clinical glucose reduction (ICMR trial) and combined effect with glibenclamide in diabetic mice.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9745215/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36366849/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"Higher risk of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Additive glucose lowering.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9745215/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"Additive glucose lowering.","mechanism":"Additive glucose lowering.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9745215/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"chirata","name":"Chirata","nameHi":"चिरायता","sanskrit":"Kiratatikta","latin":"Swertia chirata Buch.-Ham. ex Wall. (syn. S. chirayita)","otherNames":["Chiretta"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda as a bitter for general wellbeing of digestion and the liver.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Chirata churna / kwath","Mahasudarshan churna (contains chirata)","Bitter tonic blends"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Swerchirin, a chirata xanthone, strongly lowered blood glucose in rats, including tolbutamide-pretreated rats.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1891489/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Widespread adulteration/substitution of commercial chirata has been reported.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26793105/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"Could add to glucose lowering and cause low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Swerchirin lowered blood glucose in rats including those pretreated with tolbutamide; no human interaction data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1891489/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"Could add to glucose lowering.","mechanism":"Additive glucose lowering (animal data).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1891489/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"Could add to glucose lowering.","mechanism":"Additive glucose lowering (animal data).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1891489/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33118125/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"shallaki","name":"Indian frankincense / Boswellia","nameHi":"शल्लकी / सलई गुग्गुल","sanskrit":"Shallaki / Kunduru","latin":"Boswellia serrata Roxb.","otherNames":["Boswellic acids","AKBA"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of joints and mobility.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Shallaki tablets","Boswellia extract capsules (e.g. 5-Loxin, Aflapin)","Joint blends with turmeric"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Little is known about safety of medicinal oral doses in pregnancy or breastfeeding; food amounts are likely safe.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/boswellia"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Not linked to liver enzyme elevations or clinically apparent liver injury in LiverTox.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563692/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase warfarin effect and bleeding risk.","mechanism":"Boswellia serrata is listed among herbs with published warfarin interaction case reports in a review; mechanism unclear (possibly CYP inhibition).","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Check INR more often if you start or stop the herbal product.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25233607/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"minor","effect":"Could raise levels of CYP3A4-metabolised medicines.","mechanism":"Frankincense extracts were potent, non-selective inhibitors of CYP1A2, 2C8, 2C9, 2C19, 2D6 and 3A4 in vitro; boswellic acids inhibited CYP3A4 in rat microsomes.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16364338/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26762235/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Could raise levels of CYP2C9-metabolised medicines.","mechanism":"In vitro CYP2C9 inhibition by frankincense extracts.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16364338/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2d6_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Could raise levels of CYP2D6-metabolised medicines.","mechanism":"In vitro CYP2D6 inhibition by frankincense extracts.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16364338/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp1a2_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Could raise levels of CYP1A2-metabolised medicines.","mechanism":"In vitro CYP1A2 inhibition by frankincense extracts.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16364338/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"pgp_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Could alter levels of P-gp substrate drugs.","mechanism":"Boswellia extract and boswellic acids modulated P-gp transport in vitro; possible OATP1B3/MRP2 interactions.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16773534/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19010411/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May increase glibenclamide (glyburide) levels and glucose lowering.","mechanism":"Boswellic acids increased glyburide bioavailability and hypoglycaemic effect in diabetic rats.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor blood glucose.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26762235/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"vasaka","name":"Malabar nut","nameHi":"अडूसा / वासा","sanskrit":"Vasa / Vasaka","latin":"Justicia adhatoda L. (syn. Adhatoda vasica Nees)","otherNames":["Vasicine (alkaloid)"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the respiratory system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Vasavaleha","Vasarishta","Vasaka syrup / cough syrups"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"The alkaloid vasicine has oxytocic (uterine-stimulating) and abortifacient effects in animal studies; avoid in pregnancy.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/750386/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10967448/"]}],"interactions":[],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"bakuchi","name":"Babchi","nameHi":"बाकुची / बावची","sanskrit":"Bakuchi / Somaraji","latin":"Psoralea corylifolia L. (syn. Cullen corylifolium)","otherNames":["Bu Gu Zhi","Psoraleae Fructus"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the skin.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Bakuchi churna / seeds","Bakuchi oil (topical)","Somraji tailam","Skin-pigmentation blends"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Phototoxic: seeds contain psoralens; severe phototoxic burns have been reported (including in a child) when used with sun exposure. Avoid sun/UV exposure and do not combine with other photosensitising products without advice.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38387032/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33790609/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547880/"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Acute hepatitis has been reported after oral babchi seeds; constituents such as bakuchiol and psoralen are hepatotoxic in animals.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25103314/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28639266/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Seed extracts and constituents show oestrogenic activity; no human pregnancy safety data.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21382704/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to liver strain from other liver-affecting medicines.","mechanism":"Additive risk inferred from case reports of babchi hepatitis and hepatotoxic constituents in animals.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report yellow eyes/skin, dark urine or abdominal pain promptly.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25103314/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28639266/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antitubercular","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to liver strain from isoniazid/rifampicin.","mechanism":"Additive hepatotoxicity risk (theoretical), based on babchi hepatitis reports.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25103314/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"photosensitizing","severity":"moderate","effect":"Higher risk of sunburn-like skin reactions with photosensitising antibiotics (tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones).","mechanism":"Additive photosensitivity: psoralens are phototoxic, and these antibiotics are common photosensitisers.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Avoid sun exposure.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38387032/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19882789/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"minor","effect":"Could raise levels of CYP3A4-metabolised medicines.","mechanism":"P. corylifolia extract and major components inhibited CYP3A4 in recombinant enzyme and human hepatoma cell models; constituents also inhibit UGT enzymes in vitro.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28962388/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31831132/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hormonal_contraceptive_estrogen","severity":"minor","effect":"Could in theory interfere with oestrogen-containing medicines.","mechanism":"Oestrogenic activity of seed extracts and constituents in vitro.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21382704/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"bhallataka","name":"Marking nut","nameHi":"भिलावा","sanskrit":"Bhallataka","latin":"Semecarpus anacardium L.f.","otherNames":["Bhilawan","Dhobi nut"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, only after purification (shodhana), for general wellbeing of joints and digestion.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Bhallatakasava","Bhallataka avaleha","Amritabhallataka","Sanjivani vati (contains bhallataka)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"allergy","note":"The fruit's tarry oil (urushiol-like bhilawanols, anacardic acid) causes blistering contact dermatitis; it is listed as a poisonous plant under India's Drugs and Cosmetics Act and must be purified before use.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23559802/"]},{"flag":"kidney","note":"Renal cortical necrosis with anuria was reported after exposure to marking-nut sap.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/464199/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"A probable DRESS-like severe drug reaction was reported after an oral formulation containing bhallataka.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36462347/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"nephrotoxic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to kidney strain from other kidney-affecting medicines.","mechanism":"Additive risk inferred from a case of renal cortical necrosis after sap exposure; no interaction studies.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/464199/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"nsaid","severity":"minor","effect":"Possible added kidney strain with regular NSAID use.","mechanism":"Theoretical additive nephrotoxicity.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/464199/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"kuchla","name":"Nux vomica / Strychnine tree","nameHi":"कुचला","sanskrit":"Vishamushti / Kupilu","latin":"Strychnos nux-vomica L.","otherNames":["Strychnine","Brucine"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, only after purification and in very small doses, for general wellbeing of digestion and nerves.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Kupilu/kuchla (shodhit) seed preparations","Agnitundi vati","Vishatinduk vati"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Contains strychnine and brucine, highly toxic alkaloids banned or severely restricted in most countries. Poisoning causes painful muscle spasms, seizure-like episodes, rhabdomyolysis and can be fatal; strychnine poisoning has been reported from traditional/therapeutic medicines.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589896/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459306/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2634019/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27856027/"]},{"flag":"children","note":"Strychnine poisoning in a child from a therapeutic (traditional) medicine has been reported in India.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2634019/"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"LiverTox notes nux vomica has not been shown to cause liver injury; the main danger is neuromuscular toxicity.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589896/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May work against seizure control.","mechanism":"Strychnine is a convulsant (glycine-receptor antagonist) that produces seizure-like muscle spasms; theoretical opposition to antiepileptic therapy.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459306/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[{"severity":"major","effect":"The herb itself has a very narrow safety margin; combining with any medicine makes problems harder to recognise.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459306/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589896/"]}]},{"id":"vatsanabh","name":"Indian aconite","nameHi":"बच्छनाग / वत्सनाभ","sanskrit":"Vatsanabha","latin":"Aconitum ferox Wall. ex Ser.","otherNames":["Aconite","Aconitum chasmanthum (species named in the API Vatsanabha monograph)","Monkshood (related species)"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, only after purification and in minute doses, within classical compound formulations.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Shodhit vatsanabha preparations","Mahashankha vati","Sanjivani vati","Tribhuvan kirti ras","Mrityunjaya ras","Hinguleshwar ras"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Aconite alkaloids (aconitine and related) are potent heart and nerve toxins. Poisoning causes numbness, vomiting, low blood pressure, slow or chaotic heart rhythm and can be fatal; cases have followed Ayurvedic aconite preparations, especially overdose or inadequate processing.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19514874/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21170213/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25438496/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21262956/"]},{"flag":"hypotension","note":"Severe hypotension, bradycardia and AV dissociation reported after Ayurvedic vatsanabha use.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25438496/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21170213/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antiarrhythmic","severity":"moderate","effect":"Higher risk of dangerous heart rhythm problems.","mechanism":"Aconite causes ventricular arrhythmias; additive pro-arrhythmic risk with drugs that affect cardiac conduction is theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19514874/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33021540/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"moderate","effect":"Higher risk of dangerous heart rhythm problems with digoxin.","mechanism":"Both can cause arrhythmias and AV block; additive effect is theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19514874/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25438496/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"moderate","effect":"Blood pressure and pulse may fall too low (especially with beta-blockers or calcium-channel blockers).","mechanism":"Aconite toxicity causes hypotension and bradycardia; additive effect is theoretical.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25438496/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21170213/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[{"severity":"major","effect":"The herb itself has a very narrow safety margin; combining with any medicine makes toxicity harder to recognise.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19514874/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21262956/"]}]},{"id":"dhatura","name":"Thorn apple / Datura","nameHi":"धतूरा","sanskrit":"Dhattura / Kanaka","latin":"Datura metel L.","otherNames":["Datura","Devil's trumpet","Datura stramonium (jimsonweed, related)"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, only after purification and in minute doses, mainly within classical formulations.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Shodhit dhatura seed preparations","Kanakasava","Dhatura taila (topical)","Some classical rasa formulations"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Contains tropane alkaloids (scopolamine/hyoscine, hyoscyamine) causing anticholinergic poisoning: dilated pupils, confusion, hallucinations, fast heart rate, high temperature and urinary retention. Poisonings occur from herbal teas and from Datura substituted into herbal medicines.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35903511/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23378353/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30522351/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534798/"]},{"flag":"children","note":"Children are at particular risk of anticholinergic poisoning from small amounts.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534798/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30522351/"]},{"flag":"breastfeeding","note":"Scopolamine (a Datura alkaloid) may reduce milk production with long-term use.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK501482/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidepressant_tca","severity":"moderate","effect":"Stronger anticholinergic side effects: confusion, dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, fast heart rate.","mechanism":"Additive anticholinergic burden; tricyclics are strongly anticholinergic.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534798/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antipsychotic","severity":"moderate","effect":"Stronger anticholinergic side effects and risk of confusion or overheating.","mechanism":"Additive anticholinergic burden with anticholinergic antipsychotics.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534798/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"opioid","severity":"minor","effect":"More constipation, urinary retention and drowsiness/confusion.","mechanism":"Additive effects on gut motility, bladder and CNS (theoretical).","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534798/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"sedative_hypnotic","severity":"minor","effect":"More confusion or drowsiness.","mechanism":"Additive CNS effects (theoretical); benzodiazepines may be ineffective alone for Datura-induced delirium.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23378353/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anaesthesia_surgery","severity":"moderate","effect":"Unpredictable heart rate and confusion around anaesthesia.","mechanism":"Antimuscarinic alkaloids (hyoscine/atropine-like) add to anticholinergic drugs used in anaesthesia (theoretical).","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Tell your surgeon and anaesthetist about this herb; ask your doctor whether to pause the herbal product before planned surgery.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534798/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[{"severity":"major","effect":"The herb itself has a narrow safety margin and can cause delirium; combining with any medicine makes toxicity harder to recognise.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534798/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23378353/"]}]},{"id":"hing","name":"Asafoetida","nameHi":"हींग","sanskrit":"Hingu","latin":"Ferula assa-foetida L. (oleo-gum-resin)","otherNames":["heeng","devil's dung"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, usually in small culinary amounts, for general wellbeing of the digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Hing (compounded powder, usually blended with flour)","Hingvashtak churna","Hingwadi vati"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"children","note":"Case reports describe serious methaemoglobinaemia (reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood) in infants given asafoetida as a home remedy; avoid giving it to infants.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6609339/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31929780/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside warfarin-type blood thinners.","mechanism":"Asafoetida resin contains sesquiterpene coumarins; it is listed among herbs that could theoretically add to bleeding risk with warfarin. No clinical interaction reports found.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding; your INR may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10902065/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21130854/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside newer oral blood thinners.","mechanism":"Asafoetida resin contains sesquiterpene coumarins; it is listed among herbs that could theoretically add to bleeding risk with warfarin. No clinical interaction reports found.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10902065/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21130854/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to the anti-clotting effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Asafoetida resin contains sesquiterpene coumarins; it is listed among herbs that could theoretically add to bleeding risk with warfarin. No clinical interaction reports found.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10902065/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21130854/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to the blood-pressure-lowering effect of these medicines.","mechanism":"Hypotensive and smooth-muscle-relaxant activity reported in animal and laboratory studies only.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for dizziness on standing.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28725631/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"saunf","name":"Fennel","nameHi":"सौंफ","sanskrit":"Mishreya (Madhurika)","latin":"Foeniculum vulgare Mill. (fruit/seed)","otherNames":["sweet fennel","bitter fennel"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Saunf seeds / mukhwas","Fennel tea","Saunf ka arq (fennel distillate)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"The EU herbal monograph does not recommend medicinal fennel preparations during pregnancy because safety has not been established (estragole content). Normal culinary use is not covered by this caution.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-foeniculum-vulgare-miller-subsp-vulgare-var-vulgare-fructus-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"breastfeeding","note":"Not recommended in medicinal amounts while breastfeeding per the EU monograph; trans-anethole passes into breast milk.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-foeniculum-vulgare-miller-subsp-vulgare-var-vulgare-fructus-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"children","note":"EU monograph: medicinal fennel not recommended under 4 years, and limited for ages 4-12 by estragole intake. Case reports link long-term fennel tea in infants to early breast development that resolved after stopping.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-foeniculum-vulgare-miller-subsp-vulgare-var-vulgare-fructus-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18970951/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24030028/"]},{"flag":"allergy","note":"Avoid if allergic to plants of the carrot family (aniseed, caraway, celery, coriander, dill) or to mugwort pollen (cross-reactivity).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-foeniculum-vulgare-miller-subsp-vulgare-var-vulgare-fructus-revision-1_en.pdf"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antibiotic_chelating","severity":"minor","effect":"Medicinal amounts of fennel taken at the same time may reduce absorption of ciprofloxacin-type antibiotics.","mechanism":"In a rat study, fennel extract lowered ciprofloxacin peak levels by about 83% and exposure by about 48%, probably by metal-ion chelation. Animal data only; the EU monograph reports no human interactions.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Your pharmacist may suggest taking the antibiotic a few hours apart from medicinal fennel preparations.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10678493/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-foeniculum-vulgare-miller-subsp-vulgare-var-vulgare-fructus-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hormonal_contraceptive_estrogen","severity":"minor","effect":"Concentrated fennel products have weak oestrogen-like activity; the effect on hormonal medicines is unknown.","mechanism":"Anethole and related compounds are considered weakly oestrogenic; supported by traditional reputation and paediatric case reports, not by interaction studies.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6999244/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18970951/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"elaichi","name":"Green cardamom","nameHi":"इलायची","sanskrit":"Ela (Sukshmaila)","latin":"Elettaria cardamomum (L.) Maton","otherNames":["small cardamom"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive and respiratory systems.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Elaichi pods/powder","Eladi vati","Eladi churna"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside warfarin-type blood thinners.","mechanism":"Aqueous cardamom extract inhibited human platelet aggregation in the test tube; a small uncontrolled study (3 g/day) reported increased fibrinolytic activity. No clinical interaction reports found.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding; your INR may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16106388/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20361714/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside newer oral blood thinners.","mechanism":"Aqueous cardamom extract inhibited human platelet aggregation in the test tube; a small uncontrolled study (3 g/day) reported increased fibrinolytic activity. No clinical interaction reports found.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16106388/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20361714/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to the anti-clotting effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Aqueous cardamom extract inhibited human platelet aggregation in the test tube; a small uncontrolled study (3 g/day) reported increased fibrinolytic activity. No clinical interaction reports found.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16106388/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20361714/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"Larger, supplement-level amounts may slightly add to blood-pressure lowering.","mechanism":"A small 12-week open-label study (3 g/day cardamom powder, 20 people) reported lower blood pressure; culinary amounts are unlikely to matter.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Watch for dizziness on standing.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20361714/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"laung","name":"Clove","nameHi":"लौंग","sanskrit":"Lavanga","latin":"Syzygium aromaticum (L.) Merr. & L.M.Perry","otherNames":["clove oil (eugenol)"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive system and oral freshness.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Clove buds","Clove oil (dental use)","Lavangadi vati"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Clove oil (eugenol) in normal amounts has not been linked to liver injury, but swallowing large amounts, as in overdose, can cause severe liver injury.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551727/"]},{"flag":"children","note":"Accidental swallowing of clove oil by young children has caused liver failure, seizures and clotting problems; keep clove oil out of reach of children.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8215554/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1450336/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29601442/"]},{"flag":"bleeding","note":"Clove oil and its components (eugenol, acetyl eugenol) inhibit platelet aggregation in laboratory studies.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3118394/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2011614/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside warfarin-type blood thinners.","mechanism":"Eugenol and acetyl eugenol inhibit platelet aggregation and thromboxane formation in vitro; clove is listed among herbs that could theoretically add to warfarin's effect. Concentrated clove oil/supplements are of more concern than culinary use.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding; your INR may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3118394/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2011614/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38396774/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10902065/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside newer oral blood thinners.","mechanism":"Eugenol and acetyl eugenol inhibit platelet aggregation and thromboxane formation in vitro; clove is listed among herbs that could theoretically add to warfarin's effect. Concentrated clove oil/supplements are of more concern than culinary use.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3118394/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2011614/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38396774/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10902065/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to the anti-clotting effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Eugenol and acetyl eugenol inhibit platelet aggregation and thromboxane formation in vitro; clove is listed among herbs that could theoretically add to warfarin's effect. Concentrated clove oil/supplements are of more concern than culinary use.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3118394/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2011614/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38396774/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10902065/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"minor","effect":"Large amounts of clove oil can injure the liver; combining with other liver-stressing medicines could theoretically add to this.","mechanism":"Dose-related hepatotoxicity of eugenol in overdose; no interaction studies.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Avoid swallowing clove oil.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551727/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8215554/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"jaiphal","name":"Nutmeg","nameHi":"जायफल","sanskrit":"Jatiphala","latin":"Myristica fragrans Houtt. (seed)","otherNames":["mace (javitri, aril)"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda in small amounts for general wellbeing of the digestive system and for restful sleep.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Jaiphal powder","Jatiphaladi churna","Jatiphala-containing sleep or digestive blends"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Toxic in large doses: ingesting more than culinary amounts has caused hallucinations, agitation, fast heart rate, dry mouth, tremor and nausea, mainly attributed to myristicin.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24452991/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15249817/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39559662/"]},{"flag":"children","note":"Poison-centre data show unintentional nutmeg exposures in children under 13; store powders and products out of reach.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24452991/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"sedative_hypnotic","severity":"minor","effect":"Large amounts of nutmeg may add to drowsiness, confusion or other nervous-system effects of sedative medicines.","mechanism":"Psychoactive/anticholinergic-type effects of myristicin at high doses; poison-centre cases involving nutmeg plus other drugs needed more intervention. No controlled interaction studies.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Avoid more than culinary amounts.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24452991/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15249817/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticholinergic","severity":"minor","effect":"Large amounts of nutmeg may add to anticholinergic side effects (dry mouth, fast heart rate, confusion, urinary retention) of medicines such as oxybutynin, sedating antihistamines or TCAs.","mechanism":"Nutmeg poisoning produces anticholinergic-type symptoms, attributed mainly to myristicin; additive effect with anticholinergic drugs is inferred, not studied.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Avoid more than culinary amounts.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15249817/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39559662/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_maoi","severity":"minor","effect":"In theory, high-dose nutmeg could add to the effects of MAO-inhibitor medicines.","mechanism":"Myristicin and nutmeg showed monoamine oxidase inhibition in an early animal study; clinical relevance unknown.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/13994372/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp1a2_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Concentrated nutmeg products might raise levels of medicines cleared by CYP1A2 (e.g. theophylline, clozapine); culinary use is unlikely to matter.","mechanism":"Myristicin is a mechanism-based CYP1A2 inhibitor in human liver microsomes (in vitro only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26091900/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"nagarmotha","name":"Nut grass (nutsedge)","nameHi":"नागरमोथा","sanskrit":"Musta (Mustaka)","latin":"Cyperus rotundus L. (rhizome)","otherNames":["mustak","motha","nagaramusta (sometimes Cyperus scariosus)"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Mustakarishta","Musta churna","Mustadi kwath","Trimada (with chitrak and vidanga)"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Rhizome compounds inhibit alpha-glucosidase and alpha-amylase in vitro; hypoglycaemic activity reported in animal studies only.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24044731/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26167002/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Rhizome compounds inhibit alpha-glucosidase and alpha-amylase in vitro; hypoglycaemic activity reported in animal studies only.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24044731/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26167002/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Rhizome compounds inhibit alpha-glucosidase and alpha-amylase in vitro; hypoglycaemic activity reported in animal studies only.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24044731/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26167002/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside warfarin-type blood thinners.","mechanism":"Ethanol extract and its component (+)-nootkatone inhibited platelet aggregation and prolonged bleeding time in rodents; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding; your INR may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21354294/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside newer oral blood thinners.","mechanism":"Ethanol extract and its component (+)-nootkatone inhibited platelet aggregation and prolonged bleeding time in rodents; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21354294/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to the anti-clotting effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Ethanol extract and its component (+)-nootkatone inhibited platelet aggregation and prolonged bleeding time in rodents; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21354294/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"bala","name":"Country mallow","nameHi":"बला / खरेंटी","sanskrit":"Bala","latin":"Sida cordifolia L.","otherNames":["flannel weed"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general strength, vitality and wellbeing of the musculoskeletal and nervous systems.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Balarishta","Bala churna","Bala taila / Ksheerabala taila (external)","Bala-containing energy or weight-loss blends"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"May contain ephedrine alkaloids. FDA's 2004 rule banning ephedrine-alkaloid dietary supplements explicitly covers Sida cordifolia. Ephedra-type stimulants have been linked to high blood pressure, heart attack, stroke, seizure and psychosis; people with heart conditions are at higher risk.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2004-02-11/pdf/04-2912.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ephedra","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11117974/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Ephedrine-alkaloid products are considered unsafe in pregnancy and breastfeeding.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ephedra"]},{"flag":"surgery","note":"Ephedra-type stimulants can cause cardiovascular instability around anaesthesia.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11448284/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidepressant_maoi","severity":"major","effect":"May cause a dangerous rise in blood pressure (hypertensive reaction) with MAO-inhibitor antidepressants.","mechanism":"Sida cordifolia is named by FDA as a botanical source of ephedrine alkaloids; ephedrine raises blood pressure and heart rate. Alkaloid content of Bala products varies and is rarely labelled, so the size of the effect is uncertain. The ephedrine prescribing information lists MAOIs among drugs that augment its pressor effect.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Avoid this combination unless your doctor has approved it. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=e92f3eda-33eb-4b34-9e8b-db8d32a5dbd9","https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2004-02-11/pdf/04-2912.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"moderate","effect":"May raise blood pressure and work against blood-pressure medicines.","mechanism":"Sida cordifolia is named by FDA as a botanical source of ephedrine alkaloids; ephedrine raises blood pressure and heart rate. Alkaloid content of Bala products varies and is rarely labelled, so the size of the effect is uncertain. Alpha- and beta-blockers antagonise ephedrine's pressor effect per its label.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Monitor your blood pressure.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=e92f3eda-33eb-4b34-9e8b-db8d32a5dbd9","https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2004-02-11/pdf/04-2912.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11117974/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase the risk of irregular heart rhythm with digoxin.","mechanism":"Sida cordifolia is named by FDA as a botanical source of ephedrine alkaloids; ephedrine raises blood pressure and heart rate. Alkaloid content of Bala products varies and is rarely labelled, so the size of the effect is uncertain. The ephedrine label warns that combining it with cardiac glycosides may increase arrhythmia risk.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=e92f3eda-33eb-4b34-9e8b-db8d32a5dbd9","https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2004-02-11/pdf/04-2912.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"theophylline","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase nausea, nervousness and sleeplessness with theophylline.","mechanism":"Sida cordifolia is named by FDA as a botanical source of ephedrine alkaloids; ephedrine raises blood pressure and heart rate. Alkaloid content of Bala products varies and is rarely labelled, so the size of the effect is uncertain. Listed as an ephedrine interaction in its prescribing information.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=e92f3eda-33eb-4b34-9e8b-db8d32a5dbd9"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anaesthesia_surgery","severity":"moderate","effect":"May cause blood-pressure and heart-rhythm instability during anaesthesia.","mechanism":"Sida cordifolia is named by FDA as a botanical source of ephedrine alkaloids; ephedrine raises blood pressure and heart rate. Alkaloid content of Bala products varies and is rarely labelled, so the size of the effect is uncertain. Ephedra is a recognised perioperative concern.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Tell your surgeon or anaesthetist; they may advise pausing Bala products before a planned procedure.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11448284/","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=e92f3eda-33eb-4b34-9e8b-db8d32a5dbd9"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"chitrak","name":"Ceylon leadwort","nameHi":"चित्रक","sanskrit":"Chitraka","latin":"Plumbago zeylanica L. (root)","otherNames":["white leadwort"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda, after classical purification (shodhana), for general wellbeing of the digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Chitrakadi vati","Chitrak haritaki","Trimada","Chitraka churna (shodhita/purified)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Root extract showed anti-implantation and abortifacient activity in rats; avoid during pregnancy or when trying to conceive.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22118035/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Toxicity rises with plumbagin content: in rats, plumbagin-rich extracts were markedly more toxic and raised liver enzymes and urea. Classical purification is reported to lower plumbagin.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27313422/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39237121/"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Raised liver enzymes and organ-weight changes in sub-acute rat toxicity studies.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27313422/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"Could raise blood levels of narrow-margin medicines cleared by CYP3A4 (e.g. tacrolimus, cyclosporine).","mechanism":"Plumbagin inhibits CYP3A4 (and other CYPs) at low micromolar concentrations in human liver microsomes; in vitro only, human relevance unknown.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Your doctor may need to check drug levels.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27329697/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26614990/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c19_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Could raise blood levels of medicines cleared by CYP2C19 (e.g. omeprazole, citalopram) or reduce activation of clopidogrel.","mechanism":"Plumbagin inhibited CYP2C19-mediated omeprazole hydroxylation most potently of the CYPs tested (IC50 about 0.8 micromolar) in human liver microsomes; in vitro only.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26614990/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Could raise blood levels of medicines cleared by CYP2C9 (e.g. warfarin, some sulfonylureas).","mechanism":"Plumbagin was a mixed CYP2C9 inhibitor in human liver microsomes (in vitro only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27329697/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2d6_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Could raise blood levels of medicines cleared by CYP2D6.","mechanism":"Plumbagin was a mixed CYP2D6 inhibitor in human liver microsomes (in vitro only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27329697/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp1a2_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"Could raise blood levels of medicines cleared by CYP1A2.","mechanism":"Plumbagin inhibited CYP1A2 in human liver microsomes (in vitro only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27329697/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26614990/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside warfarin-type blood thinners.","mechanism":"Plumbagin inhibited platelet aggregation in vitro and after oral dosing in rats; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding; your INR may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12898414/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside newer oral blood thinners.","mechanism":"Plumbagin inhibited platelet aggregation in vitro and after oral dosing in rats; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12898414/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to the anti-clotting effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Plumbagin inhibited platelet aggregation in vitro and after oral dosing in rats; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12898414/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Plumbagin lowered blood glucose in diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22960630/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Plumbagin lowered blood glucose in diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22960630/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Plumbagin lowered blood glucose in diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22960630/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hormonal_contraceptive_estrogen","severity":"minor","effect":"May counteract oestrogen-containing medicines.","mechanism":"A plumbagin-free root extract showed anti-oestrogenic activity against ethinyl estradiol in rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22118035/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"nirgundi","name":"Five-leaved chaste tree","nameHi":"निर्गुंडी","sanskrit":"Nirgundi","latin":"Vitex negundo L. (leaf)","otherNames":[],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the joints and musculoskeletal system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Nirgundi taila (external)","Nirgundi ghan vati","Nirgundi patra kwath"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Leaf extract and an isolated iridoid glucoside lowered blood glucose in mice and diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22112721/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16253452/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Leaf extract and an isolated iridoid glucoside lowered blood glucose in mice and diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22112721/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16253452/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Leaf extract and an isolated iridoid glucoside lowered blood glucose in mice and diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22112721/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16253452/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"rasna","name":"Rasna","nameHi":"रास्ना","sanskrit":"Rasna","latin":"Pluchea lanceolata (DC.) Oliv. & Hiern (leaf)","otherNames":["Alpinia galanga and Vanda roxburghii are also sold as 'rasna' in some regions"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the joints and musculoskeletal system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Rasnadi kwath","Rasnasaptak kwath","Maharasnadi kwath","Rasnadi guggulu"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the immune-suppressing effect of these medicines.","mechanism":"Leaf extract suppressed humoral and cell-mediated immune responses and delayed graft rejection in mice (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20606832/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"devdaru","name":"Himalayan cedar (deodar)","nameHi":"देवदार","sanskrit":"Devadaru","latin":"Cedrus deodara (Roxb. ex D.Don) G.Don (heartwood)","otherNames":[],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the joints and respiratory system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Devdarvyadi kwath","Devadarvarishta","Devdaru oil (external)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Root oil given orally to rats caused irritation and erosion of the stomach and intestinal lining; avoid swallowing the essential oil.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23625432/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Antidiabetic activity is reported for extracts and fractions in animal studies, as summarised in a 2021 review; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34166738/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Antidiabetic activity is reported for extracts and fractions in animal studies, as summarised in a 2021 review; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34166738/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Antidiabetic activity is reported for extracts and fractions in animal studies, as summarised in a 2021 review; no human data.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34166738/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"jyotishmati","name":"Black oil plant / staff tree","nameHi":"मालकांगनी / ज्योतिष्मती","sanskrit":"Jyotishmati","latin":"Celastrus paniculatus Willd. (seed)","otherNames":["intellect tree"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of memory and the nervous system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Jyotishmati taila (seed oil)","Jyotishmati seed powder","Memory/brain-tonic blends"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Seed oil given by injection to rats for 30 days caused focal liver necrosis, which reversed after stopping.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2335957/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Seed oil had antispermatogenic (anti-fertility) effects in male rats; relevance to people at usual doses is unknown.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2335957/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidepressant_ssri_snri","severity":"minor","effect":"Could theoretically alter the effect of medicines that act on brain serotonin, noradrenaline or dopamine.","mechanism":"Seed oil decreased brain noradrenaline, dopamine and serotonin turnover in rats; no interaction studies.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7500635/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"vidari_kand","name":"Indian kudzu","nameHi":"विदारीकंद","sanskrit":"Vidari","latin":"Pueraria tuberosa (Willd.) DC. (tuber)","otherNames":["Ipomoea digitata is also traded as 'vidari'"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda as a rejuvenating tonic for general strength and vitality.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Vidarikand churna","Vidaryadi kwath / ghrita","Ingredient of Chyawanprash"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"The tuber is rich in isoflavones (puerarin, genistein, daidzein), which have oestrogen-like activity; people with hormone-sensitive conditions should seek advice before using concentrated products.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24980468/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"hormonal_contraceptive_estrogen","severity":"minor","effect":"Plant isoflavones could theoretically add to or compete with oestrogen-containing medicines.","mechanism":"Isoflavone phyto-oestrogen content; no interaction studies.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24980468/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticancer","severity":"minor","effect":"Phyto-oestrogens could theoretically interfere with hormone-based cancer medicines (e.g. tamoxifen, aromatase inhibitors).","mechanism":"Isoflavone phyto-oestrogen content; no interaction studies.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Tell your oncology team about all supplements.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24980468/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to the anti-clotting effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Puerarin (a major isoflavone in this tuber) inhibited platelet aggregation in early laboratory studies (studied from Pueraria lobata); no data for Vidari products.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6459876/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24980468/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"kantakari","name":"Yellow-berried nightshade","nameHi":"कटेरी / कंटकारी","sanskrit":"Kantakari","latin":"Solanum virginianum L. (syn. S. xanthocarpum Schrad. & H.Wendl.)","otherNames":["Solanum surattense"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the respiratory system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Kantakari avaleha","Kantakaryadi kwath","Vyaghri haritaki","Ingredient of Dashmool"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"diuretic_loop_thiazide","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to fluid loss and electrolyte changes with diuretic ('water') tablets.","mechanism":"Root extract (400 mg/kg) had diuretic activity comparable to furosemide in rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24266108/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Leaf extracts lowered blood glucose in diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22014732/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Leaf extracts lowered blood glucose in diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22014732/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Leaf extracts lowered blood glucose in diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22014732/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside warfarin-type blood thinners.","mechanism":"Leaf extract inhibited thrombin activity and thrombin generation in vitro at high concentrations; it did not inhibit platelet adhesion.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding; your INR may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29568195/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside newer oral blood thinners.","mechanism":"Leaf extract inhibited thrombin activity and thrombin generation in vitro at high concentrations; it did not inhibit platelet adhesion.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29568195/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"varun","name":"Three-leaved caper","nameHi":"वरुण","sanskrit":"Varuna","latin":"Crateva nurvala Buch.-Ham. (syn. Crataeva nurvala; stem bark)","otherNames":["Crateva religiosa","Crateva magna"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the urinary system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Varunadi kwath","Varunadi vati","Varun bark churna"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"sedative_hypnotic","severity":"minor","effect":"Could theoretically add to drowsiness from sedative medicines.","mechanism":"Leaf extract reduced locomotor activity and anxiety-like behaviour in mice, possibly via GABAergic pathways (animal data, leaf not the bark used in Ayurveda).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30340574/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"pashanbhed","name":"Bergenia (elephant-ears)","nameHi":"पाषाणभेद","sanskrit":"Pashanabheda","latin":"Bergenia ligulata (Wall.) Engl. (rhizome)","otherNames":["Saxifraga ligulata"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the urinary system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Pashanbhedadi kwath","Pashanbhed churna","Multi-herb urinary-wellness tablets containing pashanbhed"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"The name 'Pashanbhed' is applied to several different plants in trade (Bergenia ligulata, Kalanchoe pinnata, Coleus aromaticus, Rotula aquatica), so product identity and safety can vary.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24948861/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"diuretic_loop_thiazide","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to fluid and salt loss with diuretic ('water') tablets.","mechanism":"Rhizome extract caused diuresis with increased salt excretion in rats; plants sold as Pashanbhed also showed diuresis in rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19118615/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24948861/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"khadira","name":"Catechu (cutch)","nameHi":"कत्था / खदिर","sanskrit":"Khadira","latin":"Senegalia catechu (L.f.) P.J.H.Hurter & Mabb. (syn. Acacia catechu; heartwood extract)","otherNames":[],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the skin and oral cavity.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Khadirarishta","Khadiradi vati","Kattha (used in paan)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Flavocoxid, a prescription medical food made from Acacia catechu catechins plus Scutellaria baicalin, was linked to clinically significant (reversible) liver injury in a US DILIN case series; which component was responsible is not known.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548129/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22711078/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"minor","effect":"Concentrated catechin extracts may add to liver stress from other liver-affecting medicines.","mechanism":"Based on liver injury reported with flavocoxid (A. catechu catechins + baicalin); no data for traditional Khadira preparations.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report yellowing of the eyes or skin, dark urine or unusual tiredness.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548129/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22711078/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"iron_mineral_supplement","severity":"minor","effect":"Tannin-rich catechu taken with meals or iron tablets may reduce iron absorption.","mechanism":"Catechin and tannin-type phenolics bind non-haem iron in the gut and reduce its absorption (human iron-absorption studies of phenolics; not specific to catechu).","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Your pharmacist may suggest separating them by a couple of hours.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2598894/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"kanchnar","name":"Orchid tree (mountain ebony)","nameHi":"कचनार","sanskrit":"Kanchanara","latin":"Bauhinia variegata L. (stem bark)","otherNames":[],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the lymphatic and glandular systems.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Kanchnar guggulu","Kanchnar bark churna / kwath"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Leaf extract lowered plasma glucose in diabetic rats, and an extract and its constituent roseoside increased insulin release from beta-cells in vitro (no human data; bark is the part used in Ayurveda).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27470559/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20143296/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Leaf extract lowered plasma glucose in diabetic rats, and an extract and its constituent roseoside increased insulin release from beta-cells in vitro (no human data; bark is the part used in Ayurveda).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27470559/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20143296/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Leaf extract lowered plasma glucose in diabetic rats, and an extract and its constituent roseoside increased insulin release from beta-cells in vitro (no human data; bark is the part used in Ayurveda).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27470559/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20143296/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"bael","name":"Bael (stone apple)","nameHi":"बेल","sanskrit":"Bilva","latin":"Aegle marmelos (L.) Corrêa","otherNames":["bengal quince","wood apple (in some regions)"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Bilvadi churna","Bilva avaleha","Bael murabba / sharbat","Ingredient of Dashmool (root)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"thyroid","note":"Leaf extract and its constituent scopoletin lowered thyroid hormone levels in mice and rats; relevance to people is unknown.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12065164/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17078113/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Seed and bark extracts lowered blood glucose in normal and diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16781099/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Seed and bark extracts lowered blood glucose in normal and diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16781099/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Seed and bark extracts lowered blood glucose in normal and diabetic rats (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16781099/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"minor","effect":"Concentrated leaf products could theoretically work against thyroid-hormone replacement.","mechanism":"Leaf extract and scopoletin reduced circulating thyroid hormones in rodents (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Your doctor may want to recheck thyroid levels if you start or stop the herbal product.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12065164/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17078113/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antithyroid","severity":"minor","effect":"Concentrated leaf products could theoretically add to the effect of antithyroid medicines.","mechanism":"Leaf extract and scopoletin reduced circulating thyroid hormones in hyperthyroid rodents (animal data only).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12065164/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17078113/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"kokum","name":"Kokum","nameHi":"कोकम","sanskrit":"Vrikshamla","latin":"Garcinia indica (Thouars) Choisy (fruit rind)","otherNames":["Vrikshamla is also applied to Garcinia gummi-gutta in some texts"],"kind":"food_spice","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"Traditionally used in Ayurveda and coastal Indian cooking for general wellbeing of the digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Kokum sherbet / agal","Dried kokum rind (amsul)","Kokum butter (external)","Garcinia indica / HCA extract capsules"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Kokum contains hydroxycitric acid (HCA), the same marketed constituent as Garcinia cambogia weight-loss supplements, which have been linked to liver injury. No liver injury reports were found for kokum food use; caution applies mainly to concentrated HCA extracts.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34959738/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548087/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidepressant_ssri_snri","severity":"minor","effect":"Concentrated HCA extracts could theoretically add to serotonin effects.","mechanism":"Extrapolated from a case of serotonin toxicity with Garcinia cambogia (HCA) plus an SSRI; no data for kokum.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24699886/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34959738/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"minor","effect":"Concentrated HCA extracts could theoretically add to liver stress from liver-affecting medicines.","mechanism":"Extrapolated from liver injury with Garcinia cambogia (HCA) supplements; no data for kokum.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548087/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34959738/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"garcinia_cambogia","name":"Garcinia cambogia (Malabar tamarind)","nameHi":"गार्सिनिया कैम्बोजिया","sanskrit":null,"latin":"Garcinia gummi-gutta (L.) Roxb. (syn. G. cambogia; fruit rind extract, hydroxycitric acid)","otherNames":["brindle berry","kudampuli","HCA"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"The fruit rind is a traditional souring agent in South Indian cooking; concentrated extracts are sold as weight-management supplements.","isAyurvedic":false,"commonProducts":["Garcinia cambogia / HCA weight-management capsules","Multi-ingredient fat-burner blends (often with green tea extract)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Linked to clinically apparent acute liver injury that can be severe and occasionally fatal or require transplant, alone or combined with green tea extract.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548087/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garcinia-cambogia","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34400337/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29802521/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Case reports of mania (sometimes with psychosis) after starting Garcinia cambogia products.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27486540/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28982303/"]},{"flag":"other","note":"FDA has found weight-loss products labelled as Garcinia cambogia that contained hidden sibutramine, a withdrawn drug that can raise blood pressure and heart rate.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.fda.gov/drugs/medication-health-fraud/public-notification-fruta-planta-life-garcinia-cambogia-premium-contains-hidden-drug-ingredient"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Little is known about safety in pregnancy or breastfeeding.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garcinia-cambogia"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidepressant_ssri_snri","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase the risk of serotonin toxicity (agitation, tremor, fever, fast heart rate) with SSRI/SNRI antidepressants.","mechanism":"Case report of serotonin toxicity with Garcinia cambogia plus escitalopram; HCA is proposed to raise serotonin availability. NCCIH notes reported interactions with drugs affecting serotonin.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Seek urgent care for agitation, shaking, fever or a racing heart.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24699886/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garcinia-cambogia"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_maoi","severity":"moderate","effect":"Could theoretically increase serotonin-related side effects with MAO-inhibitor antidepressants.","mechanism":"Extrapolated from serotonergic case reports with SSRIs; no direct data.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24699886/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garcinia-cambogia"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to liver injury risk with other liver-affecting medicines (e.g. methotrexate, high-dose paracetamol, valproate).","mechanism":"Product-associated hepatotoxicity; NCCIH notes reported interactions with some drugs affecting the liver.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report yellowing of the eyes or skin, dark urine or unusual tiredness.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garcinia-cambogia","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548087/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antitubercular","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to liver injury risk with liver-affecting TB medicines (isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide).","mechanism":"Additive hepatotoxicity reasoning from Garcinia-associated liver injury; no direct reports.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548087/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garcinia-cambogia"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"green_tea_extract","name":"Green tea (extract)","nameHi":"ग्रीन टी अर्क","sanskrit":null,"latin":"Camellia sinensis (L.) Kuntze, unfermented leaf","otherNames":["EGCG","matcha"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Green tea is a traditional beverage in East Asia; concentrated extracts are sold for general wellness and weight management.","isAyurvedic":false,"commonProducts":["Green tea extract capsules (EGCG)","Weight-management blends containing green tea extract","Green tea / matcha beverages"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Green tea extracts (and, rarely, very large amounts of tea) have been implicated in acute liver injury, including liver failure needing transplant. EFSA found that 800 mg/day or more of EGCG from supplements raised liver enzymes.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547925/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/green-tea","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32625874/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Contains caffeine; keep caffeine intake moderate in pregnancy. The EU monograph does not recommend medicinal use in pregnancy or breastfeeding.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/green-tea","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-camellia-sinensis-l-kuntze-non-fermentatum-folium_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Caffeine-containing; EU monograph contraindicates medicinal use with high blood pressure, irregular heartbeat or stomach/duodenal ulcers, and advises against bedtime use. Extracts can raise blood pressure.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-camellia-sinensis-l-kuntze-non-fermentatum-folium_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/green-tea"]},{"flag":"thyroid","note":"EU monograph lists an overactive thyroid as a contraindication for medicinal green tea preparations (caffeine).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-camellia-sinensis-l-kuntze-non-fermentatum-folium_en.pdf"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"moderate","effect":"May sharply lower blood levels of some blood-pressure medicines (nadolol, lisinopril), making them less effective.","mechanism":"Catechins (EGCG) inhibit intestinal uptake transporters (OATP1A2); in healthy volunteers green tea cut nadolol exposure by about 85% and green tea extract cut lisinopril exposure by about two-thirds. A similar effect occurred with fexofenadine.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Avoid taking them at the same time and monitor your blood pressure.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24419562/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33048477/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37951784/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/green-tea","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35678032/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"statin","severity":"minor","effect":"May modestly lower blood levels of atorvastatin.","mechanism":"Green tea extract (300-600 mg) reduced atorvastatin exposure by about 22-24% in healthy volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31997084/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/green-tea"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"Very large amounts of green tea may weaken warfarin's effect (lower INR).","mechanism":"Case report: INR fell from 3.79 to 1.37 after drinking 0.5-1 gallon of green tea daily, attributed to vitamin K; INR recovered after stopping the tea. Extracts generally contain little vitamin K.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Keep tea intake steady; your INR may need checking if your intake changes.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10332534/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10902065/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticancer","severity":"moderate","effect":"Green tea polyphenols may block the effect of bortezomib and related boronic-acid cancer medicines.","mechanism":"EGCG binds the boronic acid group and prevented bortezomib-induced tumour cell death in cell and mouse studies (preclinical, but on-target).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Tell your oncology team before using green tea extracts during cancer treatment. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19190249/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to liver injury risk with other liver-affecting medicines.","mechanism":"Idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity of concentrated green tea catechins; EGCG at 800 mg/day or more raises transaminases.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report yellowing of the eyes or skin, dark urine or unusual tiredness.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547925/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32625874/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"minor","effect":"Heavy green tea intake might alter tacrolimus levels.","mechanism":"Single case report of supratherapeutic tacrolimus levels in a liver-transplant patient drinking large amounts of home-made green tea with orange extract; mechanism (CYP3A4/P-gp) not confirmed.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Keep intake steady; your doctor may check drug levels.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42226872/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"sedative_hypnotic","severity":"minor","effect":"Caffeine in green tea may reduce the effect of sedative medicines.","mechanism":"EU monograph: caffeine-containing preparations reduce actions of sedative substances and increase side effects of sympathomimetic drugs.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-community-herbal-monograph-camellia-sinensis-l-kuntze-non-fermentatum-folium_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"iron_mineral_supplement","severity":"minor","effect":"Tea taken with iron tablets or folic acid can reduce how much is absorbed.","mechanism":"Tea polyphenols bind non-haem iron in the gut; green tea also lowered folic acid absorption in volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Take iron or folic acid supplements a couple of hours apart from tea or green tea extract. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2598894/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18551467/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"ginseng","name":"Ginseng (Asian/Korean and American)","nameHi":"जिनसेंग","sanskrit":null,"latin":"Panax ginseng C.A.Mey.; Panax quinquefolius L. (root)","otherNames":["Korean red ginseng","not the same as ashwagandha ('Indian ginseng')"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Used in traditional East Asian medicine as a general tonic for energy and vitality.","isAyurvedic":false,"commonProducts":["Korean red ginseng capsules/extract","Ginseng in energy drinks","Multi-herb vitality blends"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"May be unsafe in pregnancy (a ginseng compound caused birth defects in animals); EU monograph does not recommend use in pregnancy or breastfeeding.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/asian-ginseng","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-panax-ginseng-camey-radix-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"children","note":"Some experts advise against use in infants and children; EU monograph says use under 18 is not established.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/asian-ginseng","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-panax-ginseng-camey-radix-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"autoimmune","note":"May worsen autoimmune disorders.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/asian-ginseng"]},{"flag":"bleeding","note":"May interfere with blood clotting.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/asian-ginseng"]},{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"May lower blood sugar.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/asian-ginseng","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10761967/"]},{"flag":"surgery","note":"Recognised perioperative concern because of effects on bleeding and blood sugar.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11448284/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"moderate","effect":"May reduce warfarin's effect (lower INR), raising clot risk.","mechanism":"Randomised trial: American ginseng reduced INR and warfarin levels in healthy volunteers; a case report describes a fall in INR with Panax ginseng.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Your INR may need checking more often if you start or stop ginseng.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15238367/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9075501/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the anti-clotting effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Panaxynol from ginseng inhibited platelet aggregation in vitro (ginsenosides had little effect); NCCIH notes ginseng may interfere with clotting.","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2923911/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/asian-ginseng"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"American ginseng reduced post-meal blood glucose in people with and without type 2 diabetes in a small randomised study; NCCIH notes Asian ginseng may lower blood sugar.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10761967/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/asian-ginseng"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"American ginseng reduced post-meal blood glucose in people with and without type 2 diabetes in a small randomised study; NCCIH notes Asian ginseng may lower blood sugar.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10761967/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/asian-ginseng"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"American ginseng reduced post-meal blood glucose in people with and without type 2 diabetes in a small randomised study; NCCIH notes Asian ginseng may lower blood sugar.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10761967/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/asian-ginseng"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_maoi","severity":"moderate","effect":"Reported to cause headache, tremor and manic-like symptoms with phenelzine.","mechanism":"Case report of interaction with phenelzine; mechanism unclear.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3597812/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"May lower blood levels of medicines cleared by CYP3A4.","mechanism":"Panax ginseng 500 mg twice daily for 28 days reduced midazolam exposure by about one-third (CYP3A induction) in healthy volunteers; P-gp was unaffected.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Your doctor may need to check drug levels.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21646440/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticancer","severity":"moderate","effect":"Reported liver injury when combined with imatinib.","mechanism":"Case report of imatinib hepatotoxicity after 3 months of Panax ginseng energy drinks, possibly via CYP3A4 effects. LiverTox notes ginseng may cause herb-drug interactions leading to liver injury.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Tell your oncology team before using ginseng. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20332334/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548200/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiretroviral","severity":"moderate","effect":"Reported liver injury when combined with raltegravir-based HIV treatment.","mechanism":"Case report of raised liver enzymes and jaundice (probable causality) in a patient on raltegravir plus lopinavir/ritonavir who took ginseng.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23092794/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"minor","effect":"May contribute to liver injury when combined with other liver-affecting medicines.","mechanism":"LiverTox: ginseng itself is not a recognised cause of liver injury but may cause interactions that lead to it.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548200/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20332334/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"minor","effect":"Could theoretically work against immune-suppressing medicines.","mechanism":"Immune-stimulating activity and possible worsening of autoimmune disorders (NCCIH); no interaction studies.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/asian-ginseng"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"minor","effect":"Ginseng can falsely raise or lower digoxin blood-test results with some laboratory methods.","mechanism":"Digoxin-like immunoreactive components in ginseng interfere with certain digoxin immunoassays (assay interference, not a true level change).","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Tell the lab and your doctor you take ginseng if your digoxin level is being checked. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16040294/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anaesthesia_surgery","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase bleeding and low-blood-sugar risk around surgery.","mechanism":"Perioperative review: bleeding and hypoglycaemia are recognised direct effects of ginseng.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Tell your surgeon or anaesthetist; they may advise pausing ginseng before a planned procedure.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11448284/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"st_johns_wort","name":"St John's wort","nameHi":"सेंट जॉन्स वॉर्ट","sanskrit":null,"latin":"Hypericum perforatum L. (herb)","otherNames":["hypericum"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"good","traditionalContext":"Used in European herbal tradition for general emotional wellbeing; included here as a reference herb because it interacts with many medicines.","isAyurvedic":false,"commonProducts":["St John's wort extract tablets/capsules","St John's wort tea","Mood-support blends containing hypericum"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Not recommended in pregnancy or breastfeeding; animal studies showed reproductive toxicity and breastfed infants may have side effects.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/st-johns-wort"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Can cause sensitivity to sunlight; avoid intense UV exposure while using it.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/st-johns-wort"]},{"flag":"children","note":"Not recommended under 18 years (insufficient data).","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf"]},{"flag":"surgery","note":"Interactions with anaesthesia medicines should be identified before elective surgery; it may need to be discontinued.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11448284/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"major","effect":"Can sharply lower cyclosporine, tacrolimus, sirolimus or everolimus levels; organ-transplant rejection has occurred.","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping. The EU monograph contraindicates these combinations.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid this combination. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Do not start or stop St John's wort without your transplant team.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10683008/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10866469/","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/st-johns-wort"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"major","effect":"Can reduce the effect of warfarin-type anticoagulants, raising clot risk.","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping. Contraindicated with coumarin-type anticoagulants in the EU monograph.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid this combination. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/st-johns-wort","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12392581/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"major","effect":"Can lower rivaroxaban/apixaban levels and reduce protection against clots.","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping. The rivaroxaban label says to avoid combined P-gp and strong CYP3A inducers such as St John's wort.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid this combination. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=10db92f9-2300-4a80-836b-673e1ae91610","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiretroviral","severity":"major","effect":"Can greatly lower levels of HIV medicines (e.g. protease inhibitors, nevirapine), risking treatment failure and resistance.","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping. Indinavir exposure fell markedly in volunteers; contraindicated with protease inhibitors and NRTIs in the EU monograph.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid this combination. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10683007/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11273226/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/st-johns-wort"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hormonal_contraceptive_estrogen","severity":"major","effect":"Can reduce the effectiveness of birth-control pills and cause breakthrough bleeding; unplanned pregnancy is possible.","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping. Lowered ethinyl estradiol/progestin exposure and increased breakthrough bleeding in controlled studies.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Use an additional non-hormonal contraceptive method if combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15914127/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14616430/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27444983/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticancer","severity":"major","effect":"Can lower levels of cancer medicines such as irinotecan, imatinib and docetaxel, making them less effective.","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping. Irinotecan active-metabolite levels fell by about 42% and imatinib exposure fell in clinical studies; contraindicated with cytostatics metabolised by these pathways.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid this combination. Tell your oncology team about all supplements.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12189228/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15470331/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/st-johns-wort"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"major","effect":"Can lower levels of narrow-margin CYP3A4 medicines, risking loss of effect.","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid this combination. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31742659/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_ssri_snri","severity":"major","effect":"May cause serotonin syndrome (agitation, sweating, fast heartbeat, fever, tremor) with SSRI/SNRI antidepressants.","mechanism":"Additive serotonergic effects; the EU monograph and NCCIH warn of serious serotonin-related side effects.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Avoid this combination. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Seek urgent care for agitation, shaking, fever or a racing heart.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/st-johns-wort"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_maoi","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to serotonin effects with MAO-inhibitor antidepressants.","mechanism":"Serotonergic activity; the EU monograph warns about combining with other serotonergic substances. No specific MAOI studies found.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidepressant_tca","severity":"moderate","effect":"Can lower levels of amitriptyline and related antidepressants.","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping. Amitriptyline is named among affected drugs.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/st-johns-wort"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"moderate","effect":"Can lower digoxin levels.","mechanism":"P-glycoprotein induction; digoxin exposure fell by about 25% after 10 days of co-treatment in volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Your doctor may need to check digoxin levels.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10546917/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"pgp_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"Can lower levels of medicines carried by P-glycoprotein (e.g. digoxin, dabigatran, fexofenadine).","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10546917/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"statin","severity":"moderate","effect":"Can lower simvastatin (and other CYP3A4-cleared statin) levels.","mechanism":"CYP3A4 induction; simvastatin acid exposure fell while pravastatin was unaffected in volunteers.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11753267/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"sedative_hypnotic","severity":"moderate","effect":"Can lower levels of midazolam, alprazolam and diazepam, reducing their effect.","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"opioid","severity":"moderate","effect":"Can lower methadone and oxycodone levels, reducing pain relief or triggering withdrawal.","mechanism":"CYP3A4 induction; oxycodone exposure fell markedly in volunteers and methadone levels dropped in patients.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20106684/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12649774/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"theophylline","severity":"moderate","effect":"Can lower theophylline levels, reducing its effect.","mechanism":"Induction of CYP1A2; identified as a clinically significant interaction in a regulatory/systematic review of case reports.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Your doctor may need to check theophylline levels, especially if you stop St John's wort.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12392581/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c19_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"Can lower levels of medicines cleared by CYP2C19 (e.g. omeprazole, some antidepressants) and may increase activation of clopidogrel.","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping. The EU monograph lists CYP2C19 among the induced enzymes.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31742659/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"photosensitizing","severity":"moderate","effect":"May add to sun sensitivity (burning, rash) caused by photosensitising medicines such as tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, thiazides, isotretinoin or amiodarone.","mechanism":"Hypericin in St John's wort causes photosensitivity; the EU monograph advises avoiding intense UV exposure and NCCIH notes severe skin reactions after sun exposure. Additive effect with photosensitising drugs is inferred.","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Use sun protection and avoid intense UV exposure.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/st-johns-wort"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"Can lower levels of medicines cleared by CYP2C9.","mechanism":"St John's wort induces CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and P-glycoprotein, lowering blood levels of many medicines; the effect rises with hyperforin content and lasts about a week after stopping.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"moderate","effect":"May increase the antiplatelet effect of clopidogrel.","mechanism":"Induction of CYP enzymes that activate clopidogrel; increased platelet inhibition in clopidogrel low-responders in a small randomised trial.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23463297/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"hepatotoxic","severity":"minor","effect":"May increase liver toxicity of other medicines by changing how they are metabolised.","mechanism":"LiverTox: not a convincing cause of liver injury itself, but may increase hepatotoxicity of other agents via interactions.","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548880/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anaesthesia_surgery","severity":"moderate","effect":"May alter the effect of anaesthesia and other medicines used around surgery.","mechanism":"Enzyme induction affects many perioperative drugs; enzyme activity returns to normal about 1 week after stopping.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Tell your surgeon or anaesthetist; they may advise pausing St John's wort before a planned procedure.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/final-european-union-herbal-monograph-hypericum-perforatum-l-herba-revision-1_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11448284/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"sea_buckthorn","name":"Sea buckthorn","nameHi":"सी बकथॉर्न / छरमा","sanskrit":null,"latin":"Hippophae rhamnoides L. (berry, seed oil)","otherNames":["tsermang / star-bu (Sowa-Rigpa)"],"kind":"herb","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Used in Sowa-Rigpa (Amchi) and Himalayan food traditions for general wellbeing and vitality.","isAyurvedic":false,"commonProducts":["Sea buckthorn juice","Sea buckthorn berry/seed oil capsules (omega-7)","Leh berry products"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"bleeding","note":"Sea buckthorn berry oil (5 g/day for 4 weeks) reduced platelet aggregation in a small crossover study of healthy men.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11120446/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside warfarin-type blood thinners.","mechanism":"Berry oil reduced ADP-induced platelet aggregation in a small 4-week crossover study; seed fractions inhibit platelet responses in vitro. Clinical bleeding not reported.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding; your INR may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11120446/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39123591/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_doac","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to bleeding tendency alongside newer oral blood thinners.","mechanism":"Berry oil reduced ADP-induced platelet aggregation in a small 4-week crossover study; seed fractions inhibit platelet responses in vitro. Clinical bleeding not reported.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11120446/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39123591/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"May slightly add to the anti-clotting effect of antiplatelet medicines.","mechanism":"Berry oil reduced ADP-induced platelet aggregation in a small 4-week crossover study; seed fractions inhibit platelet responses in vitro. Clinical bleeding not reported.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. Report unusual bruising or bleeding.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11120446/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39123591/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Sea buckthorn fruit puree (90 mL/day for 5 weeks) slightly lowered fasting glucose versus placebo in people with impaired glucose regulation.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33917994/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Sea buckthorn fruit puree (90 mL/day for 5 weeks) slightly lowered fasting glucose versus placebo in people with impaired glucose regulation.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33917994/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"May add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of this medicine, raising the chance of low blood sugar.","mechanism":"Sea buckthorn fruit puree (90 mL/day for 5 weeks) slightly lowered fasting glucose versus placebo in people with impaired glucose regulation.","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining. If you combine them, check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33917994/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"chyawanprash","name":"Chyawanprash","nameHi":"च्यवनप्राश","sanskrit":"Chyavanaprasha","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: amla (Phyllanthus emblica) as base","sugar / jaggery","honey","ghee","sesame oil","about 40-50 herbs incl. pippali (Piper longum), ashwagandha, guduchi, dashmool roots, cardamom, cinnamon"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"A jam-like amla-based preparation traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the respiratory and immune systems.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Dabur Chyawanprash","Patanjali Chyawanprash","Baidyanath Chyawanprash","Sugar-free Chyawanprash variants"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"High sugar content: Chyawanprash is a cooked mixture of sugar, honey, ghee and amla, and is generally considered unsuitable for people who need to limit sugar; sugar-free variants exist. One review notes small studies reporting no rise in post-meal glucose, so evidence is mixed.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31035513/"]},{"flag":"heavy_metals","note":"Classical Chyawanprash is mainly herbal, but some premium variants are sold with added metal bhasmas (check the label); heavy metals have been found in a share of Ayurvedic products sold in the US. Choose tested products.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15598918/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18728265/","https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/mm5326a3.htm","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/fraudulent-products/fda-warns-about-heavy-metal-poisoning-associated-certain-unapproved-ayurvedic-drug-products"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"sugar, honey and jaggery base (product unstudied for this interaction)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31035513/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"sugar, honey and jaggery base (product unstudied for this interaction)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31035513/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"sugar, honey and jaggery base (product unstudied for this interaction)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31035513/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"may slightly add to bleeding risk with antiplatelet medicines","mechanism":"via ingredient amla: amla extract inhibited platelet aggregation in a small clinical study; amount in Chyawanprash differs","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24291054/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"may slightly add to bleeding risk with warfarin","mechanism":"via ingredient amla: antiplatelet effect (theoretical additive effect with anticoagulants)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24291054/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"triphala_churna","name":"Triphala churna","nameHi":"त्रिफला चूर्ण","sanskrit":"Triphala","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: amalaki / amla (Phyllanthus emblica)","haritaki (Terminalia chebula)","bibhitaki (Terminalia bellirica)"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"A three-fruit powder traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Triphala churna","Triphala tablets","Triphala guggulu (also contains guggul)"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Has a laxative effect in traditional use and studies; may cause loose stools.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28696777/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"may raise levels of medicines broken down by CYP3A4 (e.g. midazolam, tacrolimus)","mechanism":"product studied: Triphala inhibited CYP3A4 in human liver microsomes and increased midazolam exposure in rats","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35785236/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20883765/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp1a2_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"may raise levels of CYP1A2 substrates (e.g. theophylline, clozapine)","mechanism":"product studied: Triphala inhibited CYP1A2 in vitro and increased phenacetin exposure in rats","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35785236/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"may raise levels of CYP2C9 substrates (e.g. warfarin, some sulfonylureas)","mechanism":"product studied: CYP2C9 inhibition in human liver microsomes only","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35785236/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20883765/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"laxative_stimulant","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to laxative effect and cause diarrhoea","mechanism":"via ingredients haritaki and amla: traditional and studied laxative action","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28696777/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiplatelet","severity":"minor","effect":"may slightly add to bleeding risk with antiplatelet medicines","mechanism":"via ingredient amla: antiplatelet effect in a small clinical study","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24291054/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"dashmool","name":"Dashmool (ten roots)","nameHi":"दशमूल","sanskrit":"Dashamoola","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: bilva (Aegle marmelos)","agnimantha (Premna sp.)","shyonaka (Oroxylum indicum)","patala (Stereospermum suaveolens)","gambhari (Gmelina arborea)","shalaparni (Desmodium gangeticum)","prishniparni (Uraria picta)","brihati (Solanum indicum)","kantakari (Solanum xanthocarpum)","gokshura (Tribulus terrestris)"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"A ten-root combination traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the musculoskeletal and nervous systems.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Dashmool kwath / churna","Dashmool taila (oil)","Dashmularishta (alcohol-containing)"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"trikatu","name":"Trikatu","nameHi":"त्रिकटु","sanskrit":"Trikatu","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: black pepper / maricha (Piper nigrum)","long pepper / pippali (Piper longum)","dry ginger / shunthi (Zingiber officinale)","active marker: piperine"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"A three-pungent-spice blend traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Trikatu churna","Trikatu capsules"],"safetyFlags":[],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antitubercular","severity":"moderate","effect":"may slow absorption and lower peak levels of rifampicin","mechanism":"product studied: Trikatu reduced rifampicin Cmax in rabbits","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining with TB medicines.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10363842/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"nsaid","severity":"minor","effect":"may lower blood levels of diclofenac","mechanism":"product studied: Trikatu pretreatment decreased diclofenac serum levels in animals","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15120451/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"moderate","effect":"may raise blood levels of phenytoin","mechanism":"via ingredient black pepper and long pepper (piperine): increased phenytoin AUC and Cmax in patients with epilepsy","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16767797/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"theophylline","severity":"moderate","effect":"may raise blood levels of theophylline","mechanism":"via ingredient black pepper and long pepper (piperine): increased oral bioavailability of theophylline in healthy volunteers","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1815977/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"may raise levels of some medicines broken down by CYP3A4 (e.g. ciclosporin, tacrolimus)","mechanism":"via ingredient black pepper and long pepper (piperine): CYP3A4 inhibition in human liver microsomes","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12130727/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"pgp_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"may raise levels of P-glycoprotein substrates such as digoxin","mechanism":"via ingredient black pepper and long pepper (piperine): P-glycoprotein inhibition in Caco-2 cells","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12130727/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"sitopaladi_churna","name":"Sitopaladi churna","nameHi":"सितोपलादि चूर्ण","sanskrit":"Sitopaladi churna","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: sitopala (rock sugar) 16 parts","vamshalochana (bamboo silica) 8 parts","pippali (Piper longum) 4 parts","ela / cardamom 2 parts","tvak / cinnamon 1 part"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"A sweet powder traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the respiratory system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Dabur Sitopaladi churna","Baidyanath Sitopaladi churna","Patanjali Divya Sitopaladi churna"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"About half of the powder by weight is sugar (sitopala); people limiting sugar should ask a doctor.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23284216/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"rock sugar is the main ingredient (product unstudied)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23284216/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"rock sugar is the main ingredient (product unstudied)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23284216/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"rock sugar is the main ingredient (product unstudied)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23284216/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"minor","effect":"may raise blood levels of phenytoin","mechanism":"via ingredient pippali (long pepper) (piperine): increased phenytoin AUC and Cmax in patients with epilepsy","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16767797/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"theophylline","severity":"minor","effect":"may raise blood levels of theophylline","mechanism":"via ingredient pippali (long pepper) (piperine): increased oral bioavailability of theophylline in healthy volunteers","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1815977/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"talisadi_churna","name":"Talisadi churna","nameHi":"तालीसादि चूर्ण","sanskrit":"Talisadi churna","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: talisa patra (Abies webbiana)","pippali (Piper longum)","maricha / black pepper (Piper nigrum)","shunthi / dry ginger","tvak / cinnamon","ela / cardamom","vamshalochana","sugar (largest share, 32 parts)"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"A spice-and-sugar powder traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the respiratory and digestive systems.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Baidyanath Talisadi churna","Dabur Talisadi churna","Talisadi vati"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Sugar is the main ingredient by weight in the classical recipe; people limiting sugar should ask a doctor.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://ijapr.in/index.php/ijapr/article/download/1235/979"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"sugar is the main ingredient (product unstudied)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://ijapr.in/index.php/ijapr/article/download/1235/979"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"sugar is the main ingredient (product unstudied)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://ijapr.in/index.php/ijapr/article/download/1235/979"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"sugar is the main ingredient (product unstudied)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://ijapr.in/index.php/ijapr/article/download/1235/979"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"minor","effect":"may raise blood levels of phenytoin","mechanism":"via ingredient black pepper and long pepper (piperine): increased phenytoin AUC and Cmax in patients with epilepsy","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16767797/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"theophylline","severity":"minor","effect":"may raise blood levels of theophylline","mechanism":"via ingredient black pepper and long pepper (piperine): increased oral bioavailability of theophylline in healthy volunteers","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1815977/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"avipattikar_churna","name":"Avipattikar churna","nameHi":"अविपत्तिकर चूर्ण","sanskrit":"Avipattikara churna","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: trivrit (Operculina turpethum, about one third)","sugar (about half)","trikatu (ginger, black pepper, long pepper)","triphala (amla, haritaki, bibhitaki)","musta","vidanga","ela","tejpatra","lavanga (clove)","vida lavana (salt)"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"A digestive powder traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Baidyanath Avipattikar churna","Dabur Avipattikar churna","Patanjali Divya Avipattikar churna","Avipattikar tablets"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Roughly half sugar by weight; also contains trivrit, traditionally used as a purgative, so it may cause loose stools.","evidence":"theoretical","sources":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/359426750_Critical_Analysis_of_Formulation_andProbable_Mode_of_Action_of_Avipattikara_churna_A_Comprehensive_Review"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"laxative_stimulant","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to laxative effect and cause diarrhoea","mechanism":"via ingredients trivrit (traditional purgative) and triphala (laxative); product unstudied","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/359426750_Critical_Analysis_of_Formulation_andProbable_Mode_of_Action_of_Avipattikara_churna_A_Comprehensive_Review","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28696777/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"diuretic_loop_thiazide","severity":"minor","effect":"frequent loose stools could add to potassium loss with water pills","mechanism":"via laxative ingredients; theoretical electrolyte loss","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28696777/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"sugar is about half the formula (product unstudied)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/359426750_Critical_Analysis_of_Formulation_andProbable_Mode_of_Action_of_Avipattikara_churna_A_Comprehensive_Review"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"sugar is about half the formula (product unstudied)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/359426750_Critical_Analysis_of_Formulation_andProbable_Mode_of_Action_of_Avipattikara_churna_A_Comprehensive_Review"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"contains a large amount of sugar, which may raise blood sugar and work against diabetes medicines","mechanism":"sugar is about half the formula (product unstudied)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before using regularly; check your blood sugar if you have diabetes.","sources":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/359426750_Critical_Analysis_of_Formulation_andProbable_Mode_of_Action_of_Avipattikara_churna_A_Comprehensive_Review"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"hingvashtak_churna","name":"Hingvashtak churna","nameHi":"हिंग्वाष्टक चूर्ण","sanskrit":"Hingvashtaka churna","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: hing / asafoetida (Ferula assa-foetida)","shunthi / dry ginger","maricha / black pepper","pippali / long pepper","ajmoda","jeera (white cumin)","krishna jeera (black cumin)","saindhava lavana (rock salt)"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"A spice-and-salt powder traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Baidyanath Hingvashtak churna","Dabur Hingwashtak churna","Zandu Hingvashtak churna"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"children","note":"Not for infants: asafoetida caused methemoglobinaemia (a serious blood oxygen problem) in a 5-week-old infant.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6609339/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23055640/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Asafoetida has traditional abortifacient use and anti-fertility effects in rats; ask a doctor before use in pregnancy.","evidence":"preclinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23055640/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"anticoagulant_vka","severity":"minor","effect":"may increase the effect of warfarin","mechanism":"via ingredient hing (asafoetida): anticoagulant activity in animals and coumarin constituents; review notes it may enhance warfarin","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23055640/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to blood-pressure lowering","mechanism":"via ingredient hing: lowered blood pressure in animal studies","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23055640/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"minor","effect":"may raise blood levels of phenytoin","mechanism":"via ingredient black pepper and long pepper (piperine): increased phenytoin AUC and Cmax in patients with epilepsy","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16767797/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"theophylline","severity":"minor","effect":"may raise blood levels of theophylline","mechanism":"via ingredient black pepper and long pepper (piperine): increased oral bioavailability of theophylline in healthy volunteers","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1815977/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"mahasudarshan_churna","name":"Mahasudarshan churna","nameHi":"महासुदर्शन चूर्ण","sanskrit":"Mahasudarshana churna","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: chirata / kiratatikta (Swertia chirata, about 50%)","about 49 other herbs incl. guduchi / giloy (Tinospora cordifolia)","kutki (Picrorhiza kurroa)","triphala","haridra","daruharidra","trikatu","neem"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"A bitter multi-herb powder traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive system and seasonal resilience.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Baidyanath Mahasudarshan churna","Dabur Mahasudarshan kadha / churna","Mahasudarshan ghan vati"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Contains guduchi (giloy); a multicentre Indian study linked giloy products to liver injury, a finding disputed by the Ministry of AYUSH. Ask a doctor if you have liver disease.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35037744/","https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1733260"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of diabetes medicines and raise the risk of low blood sugar","mechanism":"via ingredients guduchi (blood glucose lowering in animals) and other bitter herbs; product unstudied","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1529024/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32527318/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of diabetes medicines and raise the risk of low blood sugar","mechanism":"via ingredients guduchi (blood glucose lowering in animals) and other bitter herbs; product unstudied","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1529024/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32527318/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of diabetes medicines and raise the risk of low blood sugar, especially with other glucose-lowering medicines","mechanism":"via ingredients guduchi (blood glucose lowering in animals) and other bitter herbs; product unstudied","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1529024/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32527318/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"ashwagandharishta","name":"Ashwagandharishta","nameHi":"अश्वगंधारिष्ट","sanskrit":"Ashvagandharishta","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: ashwagandha (Withania somnifera, main herb)","safed musli","manjistha","haritaki","haridra","daruharidra","yashtimadhu / licorice","arjuna","vacha","chitrak","dhataki flowers (ferment starter)","jaggery / honey","class: arishta (self-fermented, alcohol-containing)"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"A fermented herbal tonic traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the nervous system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Dabur Ashwagandharishta","Baidyanath Ashwagandharishta","Patanjali Divya Ashwagandharishta"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"other","note":"Contains self-generated alcohol from fermentation (arishta/asava). Tested commercial arishtas contained roughly 5-13% v/v ethanol; levels vary by brand and batch. Ashwagandharishta brands measured 5.2-8.2% (one study) and 5.8-8.4% (another). People who must avoid alcohol should ask a doctor or pharmacist.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/publications/brochures-and-fact-sheets/harmful-interactions-mixing-alcohol-with-medicines","https://www.ijp-online.com/article.asp?issn=0253-7613;year=2006;volume=38;issue=5;spage=365;epage=365;aulast=Weerasooriya","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34825559/"]},{"flag":"liver","note":"Contains ashwagandha; NIH LiverTox lists ashwagandha as a probable cause of clinically apparent liver injury.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548536/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31991029/"]},{"flag":"thyroid","note":"Ashwagandha raised thyroid hormone levels in a clinical trial; people with thyroid conditions should ask a doctor.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Ashwagandha-HealthProfessional/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28829155/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antibiotic_other","severity":"major","effect":"alcohol with metronidazole or tinidazole may cause flushing, vomiting, fast heartbeat and blood-pressure changes","mechanism":"via ingredient: self-generated alcohol (ethanol); disulfiram-like reaction with nitroimidazole antibiotics","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Avoid alcohol-containing products while taking metronidazole/tinidazole unless your doctor says otherwise; talk to your doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/publications/brochures-and-fact-sheets/harmful-interactions-mixing-alcohol-with-medicines","https://www.ijp-online.com/article.asp?issn=0253-7613;year=2006;volume=38;issue=5;spage=365;epage=365;aulast=Weerasooriya"],"onlyMolecules":["metronidazole","ornidazole","secnidazole","tinidazole"],"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"sedative_hypnotic","severity":"moderate","effect":"may increase drowsiness, dizziness and slowed breathing","mechanism":"via ingredient: self-generated alcohol (ethanol); additive central nervous system depression","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/publications/brochures-and-fact-sheets/harmful-interactions-mixing-alcohol-with-medicines","https://www.ijp-online.com/article.asp?issn=0253-7613;year=2006;volume=38;issue=5;spage=365;epage=365;aulast=Weerasooriya"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"opioid","severity":"moderate","effect":"may increase drowsiness and the risk of slowed breathing or overdose","mechanism":"via ingredient: self-generated alcohol (ethanol); additive central nervous system depression","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/publications/brochures-and-fact-sheets/harmful-interactions-mixing-alcohol-with-medicines","https://www.ijp-online.com/article.asp?issn=0253-7613;year=2006;volume=38;issue=5;spage=365;epage=365;aulast=Weerasooriya"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiepileptic","severity":"minor","effect":"may increase drowsiness and dizziness with seizure medicines","mechanism":"via ingredient: self-generated alcohol (ethanol); additive sedation","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/publications/brochures-and-fact-sheets/harmful-interactions-mixing-alcohol-with-medicines","https://www.ijp-online.com/article.asp?issn=0253-7613;year=2006;volume=38;issue=5;spage=365;epage=365;aulast=Weerasooriya"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"alcohol may cause low blood sugar with insulin","mechanism":"via ingredient: self-generated alcohol (ethanol); alcohol impairs liver glucose output","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/publications/brochures-and-fact-sheets/harmful-interactions-mixing-alcohol-with-medicines","https://www.ijp-online.com/article.asp?issn=0253-7613;year=2006;volume=38;issue=5;spage=365;epage=365;aulast=Weerasooriya"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"alcohol may cause low blood sugar or a flushing reaction with some sulfonylureas","mechanism":"via ingredient: self-generated alcohol (ethanol); alcohol impairs liver glucose output","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/publications/brochures-and-fact-sheets/harmful-interactions-mixing-alcohol-with-medicines","https://www.ijp-online.com/article.asp?issn=0253-7613;year=2006;volume=38;issue=5;spage=365;epage=365;aulast=Weerasooriya"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"moderate","effect":"may add to thyroid hormone effects","mechanism":"via ingredient ashwagandha: raised T3/T4 and lowered TSH in a clinical trial","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor before combining; thyroid levels may need checking.","sources":["https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Ashwagandha-HealthProfessional/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28829155/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"moderate","effect":"may reduce the effect of immune-suppressing medicines","mechanism":"via ingredient ashwagandha: immune-stimulating activity (theoretical interaction noted by NIH ODS)","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","s