dients: processed biotite mica (silicates of magnesium, aluminium, iron, potassium)","Krishna vajra abhraka bhasma","Abhrak bhasma sahasraputi / shataputi"],"kind":"mineral_preparation","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"A processed mica preparation traditionally used in Ayurveda as a rasayana for general wellbeing of the respiratory system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Baidyanath Abhrak bhasma","Dabur Abhrak bhasma","Abhrak bhasma 100 puti / 1000 puti"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"heavy_metals","note":"Mineral (rasa shastra) preparation; heavy metals have been found in about 40% of rasa shastra products tested in 2008. Choose tested products.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15598918/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18728265/","https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/mm5326a3.htm","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/fraudulent-products/fda-warns-about-heavy-metal-poisoning-associated-certain-unapproved-ayurvedic-drug-products","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33402266/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antibiotic_chelating","severity":"minor","effect":"may reduce absorption of fluoroquinolone and tetracycline antibiotics","mechanism":"via mineral content (Mg, Al, Fe cations) shown in characterisation studies; chelation is theoretical for this product","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist; antibiotic and mineral doses usually need to be taken hours apart.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33402266/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34866992/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of diabetes medicines and raise the risk of low blood sugar","mechanism":"product studied: Abhrak bhasma lowered blood glucose in rats","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34362606/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of diabetes medicines and raise the risk of low blood sugar","mechanism":"product studied: Abhrak bhasma lowered blood glucose in rats","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34362606/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of diabetes medicines and raise the risk of low blood sugar, especially with other glucose-lowering medicines","mechanism":"product studied: Abhrak bhasma lowered blood glucose in rats","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34362606/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"rasa_sindoor","name":"Rasa sindoor (mercuric sulphide)","nameHi":"रस सिन्दूर","sanskrit":"Rasasindura","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: purified mercury (parada) and sulphur (gandhaka), heated by kupipakva method -> red mercuric sulphide (HgS)","found in Brahmi vati, Mahayogaraj guggulu, Vatvidhwansan ras and many 'ras' products"],"kind":"mineral_preparation","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"A processed mercury-sulphur preparation used in Ayurvedic rasa shastra as a component of herbo-mineral formulations.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Baidyanath Ras sindoor","Dabur Rasa sindoor","Products labelled 'ras' or 'rasa'"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"heavy_metals","note":"Is a mercury compound by design. Mercury and lead have been detected at levels far above safety limits in rasa shastra products; a mercury-sulphide Ayurvedic remedy (Rasa Karpura) caused severe mercury poisoning in an infant.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15598918/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18728265/","https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/mm5326a3.htm","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/fraudulent-products/fda-warns-about-heavy-metal-poisoning-associated-certain-unapproved-ayurvedic-drug-products","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22441626/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41052789/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Avoid in pregnancy and breastfeeding unless a doctor advises otherwise: lead poisoning, including miscarriage, was reported in pregnant women using rasa shastra (metal-containing) Ayurvedic medicines.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/mm6133a1.htm"]},{"flag":"children","note":"Keep away from children; severe lead and mercury poisoning has been reported in children given Ayurvedic herbo-mineral remedies.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41052789/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41244251/"]},{"flag":"kidney","note":"Mercury is toxic to the kidneys; avoid with kidney disease.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22441626/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41052789/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"nephrotoxic","severity":"moderate","effect":"may add to kidney damage risk with kidney-toxic medicines","mechanism":"mercury content (mercuric sulphide); additive kidney toxicity is theoretical","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22441626/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"calcium_bhasma_pishti","name":"Calcium bhasmas and pishtis (coral, pearl, conch, gypsum)","nameHi":"प्रवाल पिष्टी / मुक्ता पिष्टी / शंख भस्म / गोदन्ती भस्म","sanskrit":"Pravala pishti, Mukta pishti, Shankha bhasma, Godanti bhasma","latin":null,"otherNames":["Praval pishti / praval bhasma (coral; calcium carbonate)","Mukta pishti / mukta shukti bhasma (pearl; calcium carbonate)","Shankh bhasma (conch shell; calcite, about 46% calcium)","Godanti bhasma (gypsum; anhydrous calcium sulphate)","Kapardika bhasma (cowrie)","also in Praval panchamrit, Kamdudha ras, Septilin (shankha bhasma)"],"kind":"mineral_preparation","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"Calcium-rich shell and mineral preparations traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the digestive system and bones.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Baidyanath Praval pishti","Dabur Shankh bhasma","Patanjali Divya Mukta pishti","Godanti bhasma"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"heavy_metals","note":"Mainly calcium salts (a conch-shell bhasma study found lead 0.78 ppm), but heavy metals have been found in a share of rasa shastra products. Choose tested products.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15598918/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18728265/","https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/mm5326a3.htm","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/fraudulent-products/fda-warns-about-heavy-metal-poisoning-associated-certain-unapproved-ayurvedic-drug-products","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30445775/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"moderate","effect":"may reduce absorption of levothyroxine","mechanism":"via ingredient calcium bhasma/pishti (calcium carbonate/oxide/sulphate): calcium binds levothyroxine in the gut","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist; levothyroxine is usually taken at least 4 hours apart from calcium.","sources":["https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Calcium-HealthProfessional/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antibiotic_chelating","severity":"moderate","effect":"may reduce absorption of fluoroquinolone and tetracycline antibiotics","mechanism":"via ingredient calcium bhasma/pishti (calcium carbonate/oxide/sulphate): calcium chelates these antibiotics","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist; take the antibiotic at least 2 hours before or after calcium.","sources":["https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Calcium-HealthProfessional/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34866992/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"lithium","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to the risk of high calcium levels with long-term lithium","mechanism":"via ingredient calcium bhasma/pishti (calcium carbonate/oxide/sulphate): lithium can cause hypercalcaemia; extra calcium may add to it","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Calcium-HealthProfessional/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"brahmi_vati","name":"Brahmi vati","nameHi":"ब्राह्मी वटी","sanskrit":"Brahmi vati","latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: brahmi (Bacopa monnieri)","shankhpushpi (Convolvulus pluricaulis)","vacha (Acorus calamus)","jatamansi (Nardostachys jatamansi)","swarna makshika bhasma (copper-iron pyrite)","rasa sindoor (mercuric sulphide)","some versions add swarna (gold) or mukta (pearl)"],"kind":"mineral_preparation","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"A brahmi-based herbo-mineral tablet traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the nervous system and memory.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Baidyanath Brahmi vati (swarna yukt)","Dhootapapeshwar Brahmi vati","KLE Brahmi vati"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"heavy_metals","note":"Classical Brahmi vati contains rasa sindoor (mercuric sulphide) and swarna makshika bhasma; check the label.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15598918/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18728265/","https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/mm5326a3.htm","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/fraudulent-products/fda-warns-about-heavy-metal-poisoning-associated-certain-unapproved-ayurvedic-drug-products","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39631219/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22441626/"]},{"flag":"pregnancy","note":"Avoid in pregnancy and breastfeeding unless a doctor advises otherwise: lead poisoning, including miscarriage, was reported in pregnant women using rasa shastra (metal-containing) Ayurvedic medicines.","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/mm6133a1.htm"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Contains vacha (Acorus calamus); calamus is prohibited from use in human food in the US because of beta-asarone.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-189/subpart-C/section-189.110"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"moderate","effect":"may raise levels of medicines broken down by CYP3A4","mechanism":"via ingredient brahmi: Bacopa extract inhibited CYP3A4 (competitive) in vitro","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24566323/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c9_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"may raise levels of CYP2C9 substrates","mechanism":"via ingredient brahmi: CYP2C9 inhibition in vitro","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24566323/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp1a2_substrate","severity":"minor","effect":"may raise levels of CYP1A2 substrates","mechanism":"via ingredient brahmi: CYP1A2 inhibition in vitro","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24566323/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp2c19_substrate","severity":"moderate","effect":"may raise levels of CYP2C19 substrates (e.g. citalopram, omeprazole) or reduce activation of clopidogrel","mechanism":"via ingredient brahmi: Bacopa extract inhibited CYP2C19 (non-competitive) in vitro, to under 10% activity at estimated gut concentrations","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24566323/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to thyroid hormone levels","mechanism":"via ingredient brahmi: raised T4 in male mice","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12065164/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antithyroid","severity":"minor","effect":"may work against antithyroid medicines","mechanism":"via ingredient brahmi: raised T4 in male mice (theoretical)","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12065164/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"himalaya_liv52","name":"Himalaya Liv.52","nameHi":"लिव","sanskrit":null,"latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: himsra / caper bush (Capparis spinosa)","kasani / chicory (Cichorium intybus)","mandur bhasma (ferric oxide calx, about 33 mg/tablet)","kakamachi / black nightshade (Solanum nigrum)","arjuna (Terminalia arjuna)","kasamarda (Cassia occidentalis)","biranjasipha / yarrow (Achillea millefolium)","jhavuka / tamarisk (Tamarix gallica)"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"A branded polyherbal product traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the liver and digestive system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Liv.52 tablets","Liv.52 DS tablets","Liv.52 syrup","Liv.52 drops"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"In a controlled trial in alcoholic liver disease, Liv.52 showed no benefit over placebo; an unpublished trial in alcoholic cirrhosis reported lower survival with Liv.52. People with liver disease should use it only under medical advice.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12499076/","https://www.mdpi.com/2036-7422/14/3/21"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"minor","effect":"may reduce absorption of levothyroxine","mechanism":"via ingredient mandur bhasma (iron oxide): iron binds levothyroxine in the gut","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; they may advise spacing doses (often at least 4 hours from levothyroxine).","sources":["https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Iron-HealthProfessional/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antibiotic_chelating","severity":"minor","effect":"may reduce absorption of fluoroquinolone and tetracycline antibiotics","mechanism":"via ingredient mandur bhasma (iron oxide): polyvalent cations chelate these antibiotics","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist; antibiotic and mineral doses usually need to be taken hours apart.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34866992/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antiparkinson_levodopa","severity":"minor","effect":"may reduce absorption and effect of levodopa","mechanism":"via ingredient mandur bhasma (iron oxide): iron may chelate levodopa in the gut; levodopa labels warn about iron-containing supplements","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; doses may need to be spaced apart.","sources":["https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Iron-HealthProfessional/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cyp3a4_substrate_narrow","severity":"minor","effect":"theoretical change in levels of medicines broken down by CYP3A4","mechanism":"via ingredient arjuna: CYP3A4 inhibition in human liver microsomes","evidence":"preclinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28962416/","https://www.dovepress.com/the-effect-of-liv52-ds-in-metabolic-dysfunction-associated-fatty-liver-peer-reviewed-fulltext-article-HMER"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"himalaya_septilin","name":"Himalaya Septilin","nameHi":"सेप्टिलिन","sanskrit":null,"latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: Maharasnadi quath","guduchi / giloy (Tinospora cordifolia)","manjistha (Rubia cordifolia)","amla (Emblica officinalis)","moringa (Moringa pterygosperma)","yashtimadhu / licorice (Glycyrrhiza glabra)","guggulu (Balsamodendron mukul)","shankha bhasma (conch calcium)"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"A branded herbo-mineral product traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the immune and respiratory systems.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Septilin tablets","Septilin syrup"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"liver","note":"Contains guduchi (giloy); giloy products were linked to liver injury in an Indian multicentre study (disputed by the Ministry of AYUSH).","evidence":"case_reports","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35037744/","https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1733260"]},{"flag":"other","note":"Contains licorice; long-term use of glycyrrhizin can raise blood pressure and lower potassium.","evidence":"regulatory","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/licorice-root","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/draft-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-gycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"corticosteroid","severity":"minor","effect":"may increase corticosteroid effects and potassium loss","mechanism":"via ingredient licorice (glycyrrhizin): interaction with corticosteroids reported","evidence":"case_reports","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/licorice-root","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/draft-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-gycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"diuretic_loop_thiazide","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to potassium loss with water pills","mechanism":"via ingredient licorice: EMA monograph advises against use with diuretics because of electrolyte imbalance","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/draft-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-gycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"cardiac_glycoside","severity":"minor","effect":"low potassium may increase digoxin side effects","mechanism":"via ingredient licorice: EMA monograph advises against use with cardiac glycosides","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-monograph/draft-european-union-herbal-monograph-glycyrrhiza-glabra-l-gycyrrhiza-inflata-bat-glycyrrhiza-uralensis-fisch-radix-revision-1_en.pdf"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antihypertensive","severity":"minor","effect":"may lower the effect of propranolol and diltiazem","mechanism":"via ingredient guggulu (Commiphora wightii): single-dose study in healthy volunteers showed reduced Cmax and AUC of propranolol and diltiazem","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; your blood pressure or heart rate may need checking.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7852226/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"thyroid_hormone","severity":"minor","effect":"may reduce absorption of levothyroxine","mechanism":"via ingredient shankha bhasma (calcium carbonate/oxide/sulphate): calcium binds levothyroxine in the gut","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist; levothyroxine is usually taken at least 4 hours apart from calcium.","sources":["https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Calcium-HealthProfessional/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antibiotic_chelating","severity":"minor","effect":"may reduce absorption of fluoroquinolone and tetracycline antibiotics","mechanism":"via ingredient shankha bhasma (calcium carbonate/oxide/sulphate): calcium chelates these antibiotics","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist; take the antibiotic at least 2 hours before or after calcium.","sources":["https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Calcium-HealthProfessional/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34866992/"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"immunosuppressant","severity":"minor","effect":"theoretical reduction in the effect of immune-suppressing medicines","mechanism":"product studied as an immunomodulatory herbo-mineral formulation (animal data); interaction theoretical","evidence":"theoretical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining.","sources":["https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S138357182200002X"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null}],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"himalaya_cystone","name":"Himalaya Cystone","nameHi":"सिस्टोन","sanskrit":null,"latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: Didymocarpus pedicellata","pashanbhed (Saxifraga ligulata / Bergenia)","manjistha (Rubia cordifolia)","nagarmotha (Cyperus scariosus)","apamarga (Achyranthes aspera)","gojihva (Onosma bracteatum)","sahadevi (Vernonia cinerea)","shilajit","hajrul yahood bhasma (mineral)"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"poor","traditionalContext":"A branded herbo-mineral product traditionally used in Ayurveda for general wellbeing of the urinary system.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Cystone tablets","Cystone syrup"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"kidney","note":"In small randomised crossover and 1-year open-label studies (calcium oxalate and cystine stone formers), Cystone did not change urine chemistry or reduce stone burden. People with kidney stones or kidney disease should stay under medical care.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21419609/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21161651/"]}],"interactions":[],"herbWarnings":[]},{"id":"himalaya_diabecon","name":"Himalaya Diabecon","nameHi":"डायबेकॉन","sanskrit":null,"latin":null,"otherNames":["ingredients: meshashringi / gudmar (Gymnema sylvestre)","vijaysar / Indian kino (Pterocarpus marsupium)","shilajit","karela (Momordica charantia)","guggulu","yashtimadhu","gokshura","bhumyamalaki (DS variant lists more herbs)"],"kind":"formulation","dataQuality":"limited","traditionalContext":"A branded polyherbal product; recorded here only for blood-sugar interaction risk.","isAyurvedic":true,"commonProducts":["Diabecon tablets","Diabecon DS tablets"],"safetyFlags":[{"flag":"hypoglycaemia","note":"Contains several herbs that lower blood sugar; combining with diabetes medicines may cause low blood sugar.","evidence":"clinical","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259217/","https://diabetesjournals.org/spectrum/article/22/4/206/2405/Dietary-Supplements-for-Diabetes-An-Evaluation-of","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36174302/"]}],"interactions":[{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_insulin","severity":"moderate","effect":"may add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of diabetes medicines and raise the risk of low blood sugar","mechanism":"product studied: Diabecon with glimepiride gave additive glucose lowering in diabetic rats (no PK change); via ingredients gymnema (reduced need for diabetes drugs in clinical studies) and karela (case of interaction with chlorpropamide)","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36174302/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259217/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259216/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/85186/","https://diabetesjournals.org/spectrum/article/22/4/206/2405/Dietary-Supplements-for-Diabetes-An-Evaluation-of"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_sulfonylurea","severity":"moderate","effect":"may add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of diabetes medicines and raise the risk of low blood sugar","mechanism":"product studied: Diabecon with glimepiride gave additive glucose lowering in diabetic rats (no PK change); via ingredients gymnema (reduced need for diabetes drugs in clinical studies) and karela (case of interaction with chlorpropamide)","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36174302/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259217/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259216/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/85186/","https://diabetesjournals.org/spectrum/article/22/4/206/2405/Dietary-Supplements-for-Diabetes-An-Evaluation-of"],"onlyMolecules":null,"majorOnlyFor":null},{"drugClass":"antidiabetic_other","severity":"minor","effect":"may add to the blood-sugar-lowering effect of diabetes medicines and raise the risk of low blood sugar, especially with other glucose-lowering medicines","mechanism":"product studied: Diabecon with glimepiride gave additive glucose lowering in diabetic rats (no PK change); via ingredients gymnema (reduced need for diabetes drugs in clinical studies) and karela (case of interaction with chlorpropamide)","evidence":"clinical","advice":"Talk to your doctor or pharmacist before combining; check your blood sugar more often and know the signs of low blood sugar.","sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36174302/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2259217/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm